This is a research slate rather than a believer’s list. “Proof” here means proof that the person, investigation, performance, text, cult or reported event existed—not automatic proof that its supernatural interpretation is true.

Scoring: S = story/video potential; E = evidential strength.

    E5: instrumented or unusually strong archival evidence

    E4: good contemporary documentation

    E3: historical person, but extraordinary episodes are disputed

    E2: early lineage text or substantial hagiography

    E1: predominantly late legend

    E0: mythic figure treated as historical

I cannot prove that “nobody has heard of” someone, so I prioritized figures with large regional followings but low recognition in English-language popular culture.
I. Tested, filmed or contemporary yogic anomalies

    Swami Rama — S5/E5. Menninger researchers recorded voluntary-looking temperature changes and an episode of rapid atrial flutter during which there was reportedly no effective cardiac output for roughly seventeen seconds. “He stopped his heart” is the clickable version; “he produced an extraordinary, medically recorded arrhythmia” is the defensible version.

Prahlad Jani — S5/E2. An Indian medical team confined and observed him for fifteen days in 2010, claiming he consumed neither food nor water. The complete study was never published in a strong peer-reviewed form, and critics identified important protocol problems; this does not prove lifelong breatharianism.

Sadhu Haridas — S5/E3. Nineteenth-century accounts say he was sealed underground before Maharaja Ranjit Singh and later revived. Contemporary texts make the case historically fascinating, but the custody arrangements were not remotely equivalent to a modern controlled experiment.

Yogi Satyamurti — S5/E5. Researchers monitored him during eight days inside a sealed underground enclosure and published physiological observations in a medical journal. This is probably your best genuinely obscure “buried yogi studied by scientists” story.

Kuda Bux — S5/E4. A Kashmiri performer became internationally known for firewalking and apparently seeing while heavily blindfolded. The performances are well documented, although ordinary physical and conjuring explanations are much stronger than supernatural ones.

T. Krishnamacharya — S4/E4. Before becoming “the father of modern yoga,” he staged palace demonstrations involving unusual postures, strength and claimed pulse control. Archival footage makes this far more visually usable than most siddha legends.

Pilot Baba — S5/E2. A former military pilot turned guru who claimed prolonged underground samādhi demonstrations. His career also accumulated fraud allegations and serious controversies, making the real story one of charisma, danger and the manufacture of miraculous authority.

Devraha Baba — S4/E3. A nearly unclothed ascetic who lived on an elevated wooden platform and received politicians, officials and enormous crowds. His public presence is well documented; claims that he lived for centuries are not.

Shivapuri Baba — S5/E2. Tradition gives him a 137-year lifespan, worldwide travels and encounters with major political figures before his final years in Nepal. The travel narrative is researchable, but the extreme age rests mainly on devotional biography.

Tapaswiji Maharaj — S5/E1. A royal soldier supposedly became a Himalayan ascetic and lived for around 185 years through rejuvenation disciplines. Almost all of the extraordinary material comes from his spiritual biography rather than independent records.

These historical feats are research subjects, not things to reproduce: fasting without fluids, burial, cardiac manipulation and firewalking can be fatal.
