    Pāmbāṭṭi Siddhar — S5/E2. A snake-catcher searching for the ultimate serpent is redirected toward the “snake” of consciousness within; his poems repeatedly call for it to dance.

    Rāmadevar/Yacob — S5/E1. Tamil traditions say he traveled westward, entered another body, lived among Arabs as Yacob and returned with foreign medical knowledge.

    Kudambai Siddhar — S4/E2. A radically anti-ceremonial poet whose songs mock external religious status and ask what use ritual signs have to someone who knows awareness directly.

    Sivavakkiyar — S5/E3. A fierce anti-caste, anti-idolatry poet who attacks purity rules while using body, sexuality and birth as philosophical weapons.

    Pattinathar — S5/E3. A fabulously rich merchant who renounces everything after receiving a needle without an eye and the message that not even that useless object can accompany him after death.

    Pulippāṇi Siddhar — S4/E1. Bogar’s disciple, remembered as a wilderness adept who rides a tiger and guards his teacher’s pharmacological and alchemical knowledge.

    Kākabhujandar/Kāga Bhusundi — S5/E0. An immortal crow-yogi who supposedly survives repeated destructions of the universe and reports cosmic history from an animal body.

