1. Yogi Satyamurti — the medically monitored underground confinement
Safe evidence framing

There really was a medical observation associated with Yogi Satyamurti, but the defensible story is:

    Researchers monitored an unusual extended confinement and recorded physiological changes; the evidence does not establish a supernatural mechanism, and the event should not be reproduced.

Do not title it simply “The Yogi Who Survived Without Oxygen” because the instrumentation, air conditions and exact metabolic interpretation are more complicated than that. A stronger critical title would be:

Scientists Sealed a Yogi Underground for Eight Days—What Did They Actually Record?

For visuals, use:

    photographs of Indian medical laboratories from the period;

    generic ECG machines;

    journal pages;

    newspaper typography;

    exterior shots of Rajasthan or Jaipur;

    a diagram showing what measurements were and were not available.

Do not reenact confinement or show instructional demonstrations.
2. Swami Rama at the Menninger Foundation
Safe evidence framing

The phrase “Swami Rama stopped his heart” is misleading. The Menninger descriptions are more consistent with an induced rapid abnormal rhythm that briefly produced little or no effective pulse, rather than a clean, motionless cardiac arrest. Atrial flutter is itself a potentially dangerous rhythm, so this should be presented as a historical biofeedback experiment, not an attainable yogic exercise.

The best angle:

Did Swami Rama Really Stop His Heart? Reading the Menninger Records Carefully

Useful narrative structure:

    The late-1960s biofeedback movement.

    Elmer and Alyce Green’s Voluntary Controls Program.

    Swami Rama’s arrival.

    Temperature differentiation in the hand.

    Brain-wave demonstrations.

    The cardiac recording.

    What “no pulse” does and does not mean.

    How later spiritual biographies simplified the event.

Use generic laboratory, biofeedback and archival stills rather than demonstrations of inducing abnormal heart rhythms.
3. Sadhu Haridas and Maharaja Ranjit Singh
Safe evidence framing

Haridas belongs to a long nineteenth-century debate over yogic trance, suspended animation and colonial interpretations of Indian asceticism. The accounts are historically important, but the security arrangements did not meet modern experimental standards, and later retellings often magnified the story.

Best title:

The Yogi Buried at Ranjit Singh’s Court: Miracle, Trance or Colonial Myth?

Build it around:

    the Sikh court at Lahore;

    European officers serving Ranjit Singh;

    nineteenth-century fascination with apparent suspended animation;

    differing versions of the duration and custody arrangements;

    how later writers transformed a court spectacle into “scientific proof.”

Use paintings of Ranjit Singh’s court, maps of Lahore and pages from nineteenth-century travel narratives. Do not include reenactments or footage presenting live burial as a feat.
4. Bogar/Boganathar — Tamil siddha, alchemist and architect of legend
Evidence position

Bogar is attached to a real and extensive Tamil textual tradition, but his date and biography are extremely unstable. The connection to the Palani image is established as an important temple tradition; the stories that he traveled to China, became Laozi or produced an immortal body are much later and harder to verify. David Gordon White is the essential academic starting point for understanding siddha alchemy without simply accepting modern miracle claims.
