Research Pack 01: Kāṇha, the Dark Siddha Who May Never Have Been One Person
Core video thesis

Kāṇha—also called Kanhapa, Kṛṣṇācārya, Kṛṣṇapāda and “the Dark Siddha”—appears as an early vernacular poet, a Buddhist tantric master, a proto-Nāth yogi and a Tibetan mahāsiddha. The most interesting possibility is that these traditions fused at least two historical adepts into a single supernatural biography.

This is not simply a “forgotten magician” story. It is an investigation into how a real authorial name moved through:

    eastern Indian Buddhist monasteries;

    vernacular tantric song;

    Nāth yogic memory;

    Newar Buddhist ritual performance;

    Tibetan lineage histories;

    later Bengali claims about the origins of Bengali literature.

A recent Berkeley study explicitly distinguishes an elder Kṛṣṇācārya associated with Jālandharī and Cakrasaṃvara from a younger Kṛṣṇa/Kṛṣṇavajra associated with Hevajra and the Sakya Lamdré lineage. The same study warns that even the thirteen songs attributed to Kāṇha may not all have been written by one person.
