The opening hook

    One of the first great poets of eastern India was remembered not merely as a poet, but as a skull-bearing yogi who flew beneath supernatural parasols, commanded an army of disciples and died after failing to recognize a woman as a ḍākinī.

    But Tibetan scholars preserved a disturbing possibility: there may have been two Kāṇhas. Perhaps even more.

Visually cut between:

    Tibetan painting of Kanhapa.

    palm-leaf manuscript texture;

    the name Kāṇha appearing in Bengali, Sanskrit and Tibetan transliteration;

    a map linking Bengal, Odisha, Bihar, Nepal and Tibet;

    two silhouettes splitting from one name.

Why this subject is unusually strong

The story contains several distinct content engines:

    identity mystery: one siddha or several;

    religious crossover: Buddhist, Nāth and regional literary traditions;

    lost-language mystery: songs composed in an intentionally difficult idiom;

    social transgression: dialogue with a ḍombī woman situated outside respectable urban society;

    spiritual failure: enormous powers paired with imperfect recognition;

    living survival: related ritual songs continue in Newar Buddhist settings;

    literary nationalism: Bengali and other eastern Indian traditions claim the Charyāpada as an ancestral literature.

The figure therefore supports one flagship documentary and several shorter videos.
Evidence assessment
Grade A: strong textual evidence
A historical authorial name existed

Forms of the name Kāṇha/Kanhapa/Kṛṣṇācārya occur in colophons, song signatures, tantric textual traditions and Tibetan canonical materials. This does not prove that every text belongs to one author, but it strongly supports the existence of one or more influential masters using the name.
Songs are attributed to Kāṇha

Munidatta’s Charyā manuscript attributes thirteen songs to Kāṇha, more than to any other named poet in that collection. Twelve survive in an eastern Indo-Aryan form, while another is known through Tibetan transmission.
Kṛṣṇācārya was important to Cakrasaṃvara transmission

Textual and Tibetan lineage sources associate an elder Kṛṣṇācārya with Jālandharī and Cakrasaṃvara/Heruka literature. Another similarly named master is associated with Hevajra commentarial traditions and the Sakya Lamdré lineage.
His identity was already confused inside Tibetan traditions

Himalayan Art’s iconographic research distinguishes Kāṇha of the East from the more widely represented Kṛṣṇācārya. The names are frequently conflated in museum descriptions and modern summaries.
Grade B: plausible reconstruction
He probably belonged to eastern India

The language of the songs and their relationship to eastern Buddhist traditions strongly indicate eastern India. Claims range across Bengal, Bihar and Odisha, but a precise birthplace remains uncertain.

Banglapedia places Kāṇha approximately in the tenth century and associates him with the Pāla-period world. It identifies Kṛṣṇācāryapāda as the full form behind Kāṇha and related vernacular names.
He may have been connected with Jālandharī/Jālandharipa

Multiple Buddhist hagiographic and textual traditions identify Jālandharī as the elder Kṛṣṇācārya’s teacher. This relationship is stronger than many details in the miracle biography, though it still comes through lineage literature rather than contemporary inscriptional evidence.
Some of the Kāṇha material entered Nāth memory

Later accounts position Kanhapa near the formative Nāth siddhas, especially through his connection with Jālandharī. However, saying he was straightforwardly a “Nāth Hindu yogi” imposes later institutional boundaries upon a period when tantric Buddhist and Śaiva yogic milieus overlapped.
Grade C: hagiographic but historically revealing

These motifs should be treated as sacred biography:

    supernatural travel;

    flying parasols and drums;

    mastery of powerful rites;

    battles with ḍākinīs;

    commanding hundreds of disciples;

    miraculous control over fruit or trees;

    death after failing to recognize an apparently ordinary woman;

    liberation occurring after physical death.

They are valuable evidence for how later communities understood tantric authority, pride and feminine wisdom—but not reliable event reportage.
Grade D: claims to avoid stating as fact

    Kāṇha was born in a specific modern Bangladeshi village.

    He lived at Somapura Mahāvihāra.

    Every Kāṇha song was written by one historical person.

    He was unquestionably the same person as Kṛṣṇasamayavajra.

    He was formally a member of the later Nāth Sampradāya.

    He literally flew with seven parasols.

    He commanded exactly seven hundred disciples.

    His legendary final encounter occurred exactly as later biographies narrate it.

    The Charyāpada is exclusively “the first Bengali book.”

Several eastern Indo-Aryan language traditions legitimately study the collection as part of their early linguistic history.
The identity problem
Candidate 1: Elder Kṛṣṇācārya

This figure is usually described as:

    disciple of Jālandharī;

    teacher in a Vajravārāhī or Cakrasaṃvara environment;

    associated with the elder Kāṇha biography;

    possibly connected with Mekhalā and Kanakhalā;

    author or attributed author of Cakrasaṃvara literature;

    represented riding or accompanied by a vetāla in some iconographic traditions.

The Berkeley study connects this elder figure with the Vasantatilakā and describes him as the Kāṇha represented with flying parasols and drums.
Candidate 2: Younger Kāṇha/Kṛṣṇavajra

This figure is associated with:

    eastern India;

    Virūpa;

    Hevajra;

    the Mārgaphala or Lamdré transmission;

    the Yogaratnamālā commentary;

    Sakya lineage history.

The two masters may have been separated by lineage function rather than by clean historical memory. Tibetan traditions inherited similar names, overlapping iconography and texts whose authorship was already uncertain.
Candidate 3: The poet “Kāṇha”

The Kāṇha of the Charyā songs could be:

    the elder Kṛṣṇācārya;

    a combination of both;

    an authorial persona used by multiple composers;

    a prestigious siddha to whom later songs were attributed.

The signature at the end of a song is not equivalent to a modern verified author credit. The Berkeley dissertation notes that attribution depends largely upon each song’s bhaṇitā, or signature verse, leaving open the possibility of later composition under a famous siddha’s name.

This is the documentary’s key reveal.
The songs and “twilight language”
What the Charyā songs are

The Charyā corpus is a collection of esoteric Buddhist songs surviving with Sanskrit commentary and Tibetan parallels. Their surface scenes involve boats, villages, women, hunting, domestic labor, rivers, outcastes and wandering yogis.

Under the surface, commentators read them as encoding:

    stages of tantric realization;

    yogic embodiment;

    nonduality;

    bliss and emptiness;

    channels, winds or inner processes;

    antinomian ritual symbolism;

    critiques of conventional religious identity.

Calling this simply a “secret code” is too crude. The songs work through layered metaphor, polyvalence, technical vocabulary, vernacular speech and performance.
Kāṇha’s ḍombī song

One of Kāṇha’s best-known songs addresses a ḍombī woman whose dwelling lies outside the city. The social geography matters:

    city versus margin;

    purity versus pollution;

    respectable religion versus charnel-ground knowledge;

    male adept versus socially excluded woman;

    literal encounter versus interior yogic symbolism.

A sophisticated treatment should not flatten the woman into merely a metaphor. The poem uses a real hierarchy of caste and occupation even while transfiguring the marginal figure into a bearer of esoteric truth.
Sanskrit formulation

सन्ध्याभाषा — sandhyābhāṣā

Often translated as “twilight language,” although scholars debate the exact historical sense.

A useful narration line:

    The language does not merely hide meaning. It places meaning at a threshold, where social scene, ritual instruction and realization occupy the same words.

The dark legend

Tāranātha’s biography, translated by David Templeman, is the major English source for Kāṇha’s full legendary life. It was compiled centuries after the siddha’s presumed lifetime from written and oral materials. The book covers his training under Jālandharipa, journeys, encounters and “untimely death.”
Responsible summary

At the height of his reputation, Kāṇha travels surrounded by signs of accomplishment. Yet his apparent realization remains incomplete. In a decisive encounter, he fails to recognize feminine wisdom appearing in socially ordinary or marginal form. Pride and aggressive use of power lead to his downfall.

The deepest theme is:

    Siddhi without recognition is not liberation.

This is more interesting than sensationalizing the confrontation itself.
Why the story matters

The legend attacks the very thing it appears to celebrate:

    Kāṇha possesses powers, yet lacks complete wisdom.

    He attracts disciples, yet cannot recognize the sacred when it refuses obedience.

    He masters tantric systems, yet remains vulnerable to pride.

    The socially marginal woman becomes spiritually superior to the famous male adept.

That gives the story critical force rather than mere occult spectacle.
Newar Nepal: where the songs may still live

One of the strongest underused angles is the survival of cacā or caryā performance among Newar Buddhist communities in the Kathmandu Valley.

The Berkeley research notes that ritual performance of cacā songs continues in the valley and that some surviving songs retain Kāṇha’s signature. It also reports local confusion between Kāṇha/Kṛṣṇācārya and a similarly named Karṇaripa, showing that identity fluidity exists inside the living tradition itself.
