Recommended documentary structure
Act I — The poet with too many names

Introduce:

    Kāṇha;

    Kanhapa;

    Kṛṣṇapāda;

    Kṛṣṇācārya;

    Nagpopa;

    the Dark Siddha.

Show how a simple search produces mutually contradictory biographies.

Question: Are these spelling variations—or different people?
Act II — Songs from the end of Indian Buddhism

Set the Pāla-era environment:

    large monasteries;

    mobile tantric teachers;

    Sanskrit scholasticism;

    vernacular performance;

    Buddhist and Śaiva tantric exchange;

    eastern India as a major centre rather than a peripheral zone.

Introduce the Charyā songs.
Act III — The woman outside the city

Analyse one Kāṇha song without disclosing restricted ritual interpretation as definitive.

Focus on:

    social geography;

    caste marginality;

    feminine wisdom;

    ambiguity;

    why tantric poets placed truth outside respectable space.

Act IV — The siddha becomes supernatural

Shift to Tāranātha’s much later biography.

Present the flying parasols, powers and massive following as features of sacred biography. Ask what the narrative is designed to establish.
Act V — Spiritual power fails

Tell the final encounter in restrained terms.

Core line:

    Kāṇha’s tragedy was not that he lacked power. It was that power convinced him he could no longer fail to recognize wisdom.

Act VI — The man splits in two

Reveal elder Kṛṣṇācārya versus younger Kṛṣṇavajra/Kāṇha of the East.

Use an attribution board:
Feature	Elder Kṛṣṇācārya	Younger Kāṇha
Teacher	Jālandharī	associated with Virūpa
Primary cycle	Cakrasaṃvara	Hevajra
Tibetan reception	broad Cakrasaṃvara lineages	Sakya/Lamdré
Texts	Vasantatilakā and related works	Yogaratnamālā and Hevajra works
Charyā songs	possible	possible/confused

Stress that this remains a scholarly reconstruction, not a solved identification.
Act VII — A voice surviving in Nepal

End in the Kathmandu Valley.

The poet may be historically inaccessible, yet the ritual technology of song, mask, gesture and embodied deity continues in transformed Newar traditions.

Final line:

    Kāṇha disappeared as a person because he survived as a lineage, a song, a mask and a name that too many traditions needed to surrender.

