Important cultural caution

Some Newar tantric performances are public cultural presentations; others belong to restricted ritual contexts. Permission to film a public dance does not imply permission to describe private ritual meanings or reproduce restricted liturgical material.
Misleading framings to avoid
“The first Bengali poet”

Better:

    One of the major named poets in a corpus claimed within the early literary history of several eastern Indo-Aryan languages.

“He wrote in secret code to hide sex magic”

Better:

    His songs use deliberately multivalent language in which ordinary social scenes, ritual symbolism and contemplative realization overlap.

“A Hindu Nāth saint stolen by Buddhists”

The chronology and categories are much less tidy. Buddhist siddha and Nāth traditions developed through overlapping regional yogic environments.
“A Buddhist sorcerer killed by a witch”

This strips the account of its religious argument and turns a ḍākinī into a horror stereotype.
“Ancient proof of supernatural powers”

The miracle biography was written long after Kāṇha’s presumed period and serves lineage, doctrinal and moral functions.
“Kāṇha was definitely one person”

This is exactly what the strongest recent scholarship questions.
Unresolved questions worth pursuing

    Which of the thirteen Kāṇha songs share distinctive vocabulary with the Vasantatilakā?

    Can stylometry distinguish multiple Kāṇha authors?

    Did the elder-versus-younger distinction originate in India or in Tibetan lineage organization?

    Which Kāṇha songs survive in Newar cacā performance?

    Are living Newar attributions based upon manuscripts or oral teacher lineages?

    When was Kāṇha first incorporated into Nāth lists?

    Does early Nāth literature preserve any songs independently attributed to Kanhapa?

    What is the earliest source connecting him with Somapura?

    When did the specific Netrokona birthplace tradition arise?

    Are museum images labelled Kanhapa iconographically consistent with the elder or younger master?

    Did sectarian discomfort with transgressive practice cause one historical identity to be divided between lineages?

    Can the hagiographic final encounter be mapped onto imagery inside texts attributed to him?

The Berkeley study itself raises the possibility that one historical siddha’s identity was split because different communities transmitted different texts and selectively retained or censored transgressive elements. It presents this as an open question rather than a conclusion.
