Narrative structure
Act I — The genius myth

Introduce the familiar Abhinavagupta:

    polymath;

    philosopher;

    aesthete;

    tantric authority;

    author of the Tantrāloka.

Then break the image:

    Abhinavagupta did not claim to have invented the system alone.

Show his network of teachers.
Act II — Fifteen teachers, one decisive master

Briefly introduce:

    Lakṣmaṇagupta and Pratyabhijñā;

    Bhūtirāja;

    dualist Śaiva teachers;

    grammarians and literary teachers;

    Buddhist and Vaiṣṇava learning;

    Śambhunātha as the culminating Kaula guru.

The point is not to recite all names. It is to show that Abhinavagupta intentionally built himself through plural instruction.
Act III — The master from Jālandhara

Map the uncertain sacred geography.

Use conditional language:

    associated with;

    linked to;

    possibly from;

    representing the Jālandhara current.

Do not fabricate a journey with exact roads and dates.
Act IV — Scripture needs a living mouth

Explain guru-mediated revelation.

Visualize:

    a text;

    a reader;

    conflicting interpretations;

    lineage commentary;

    experiential recognition.

