Research Pack 03: Jñānanetra and the Cremation-Ground Goddesses of Uḍḍiyāna
Core video thesis

Before Abhinavagupta transformed Kashmir Śaivism into one of India’s most sophisticated philosophical systems, an obscure ninth-century master called Jñānanetra claimed to receive an entire tradition directly from female yoginīs in a cremation ground beyond Kashmir.

He then reportedly entrusted its highest transmission not to famous male scholars, but to three women—especially Keyūravatī, remembered simply as “the Queen” or “the Goddess.”

This is the lost origin story of the Krama, the Śaiva tradition that taught that perception itself unfolds as a sequence of Kālīs:

    appearing;

    being grasped;

    dissolving;

    vanishing into awareness;

    and revealing the consciousness that consumed the entire sequence.

The flagship investigation is therefore:

    Did Jñānanetra found a historical female-led tantric lineage—or did later Kashmiri authors construct a revelation myth to authorize teachings inherited from the mysterious region of Uḍḍiyāna?

The strongest evidence supports a real Jñānanetra or Śivānanda active around the ninth century, a recognized lineage descending from him, and Krama scriptures and hymns connected with his name. The yoginī initiation, precise cremation-ground location and direct supernatural revelation belong primarily to lineage tradition.
