Thumbnail text:

THE GODDESSES TAUGHT HIM

Alternative:

BEFORE ABHINAVAGUPTA

Use a manuscript or painted Kālī image rather than a graphic cremation-ground reenactment.
Opening hook

    Around the middle of the ninth century, a Kashmiri seeker is said to have crossed the mountains into Uḍḍiyāna—a land that both Buddhist and Śaiva tantrics remembered as a source of forbidden revelation.

    In a cremation ground called Kāravīra, he encountered a circle of yoginīs.

    They did not give him a book. They made him the first human carrier of a new revelation: a system in which every moment of seeing, thinking and forgetting was the activity of Kālī.

    His name was Jñānanetra. His successor was a woman. And almost everything else about them disappeared.

Opening visual sequence:

    Snow-covered passes north-west of Kashmir.

    Map moving toward the Swat Valley.

    Śāradā-script manuscript texture.

    A circle of twelve names appearing around a dark centre.

    Jñānanetra’s name.

    Three female silhouettes replacing him.

    Text: “WHY WAS THIS LINEAGE FORGOTTEN?”

Why this story is valuable

This subject combines several high-signal themes:

    a nearly forgotten teacher behind Abhinavagupta;

    Uḍḍiyāna, shared by Buddhist and Śaiva tantric origin stories;

    goddess revelation;

    cremation-ground mythology;

    female lineage holders;

    lost scriptures;

    Old Kashmiri oral material;

    Kālī interpreted as the structure of cognition;

    historical overlap between philosophy, ritual and visionary authority;

    a line of transmission that moved from Kashmir into South India.

It also gives you a way to explain one of Abhinavagupta’s most powerful ideas without beginning with dense metaphysics:

    Every experience is born, stabilized and consumed—and the power carrying it through those stages is Kālī.

Who was Jñānanetra?
Names

He appears under several related names:

    Jñānanetra

    Jñānanetranātha

    Netra

    Śivānanda

    Śivānandanātha

    sometimes Siddhanātha in modern book titles or interpretive traditions

The name Jñānanetra means approximately:

ज्ञाननेत्र — jñānanetra
“Eye of knowledge” or “eye of wisdom.”

शिवानन्द — śivānanda
“Bliss of Śiva.”

Researchers generally place him around the middle or latter half of the ninth century. Aleksandra Wenta’s work cites Jayaratha’s description of him as the master who “brought down” or introduced the Krama teaching into the human world.
What is historically plausible

A teacher called Jñānanetra or Śivānanda probably stood near the beginning of the historically traceable Krama lineage.

He was remembered as:

    the first human promulgator of the system;

    a transmitter of early Krama scriptures;

    a composer or attributed composer of a Kālī hymn;

    teacher of a group that included important women;

    a source invoked by later Krama authors;

    the human link between Uḍḍiyāna revelation and Kashmiri transmission.

What remains unknown

We do not securely know:

    his birthplace;

    his family;

    his exact caste or social position;

    where in Kashmir he lived;

    whether he physically travelled to Swat;

    which surviving text is unquestionably his;

    whether “Jñānanetra” was an initiatory title;

    whether different figures were merged under the name;

    how large his community was;

    how the lineage was institutionally organized.

He is historically visible mainly because later writers needed to establish the line through which revelation reached them.
Evidence grading
Grade A — securely attested tradition and textual reception
Jñānanetra was regarded as the first human Krama teacher

Jayaratha, the major commentator on Abhinavagupta’s Tantrāloka, identifies Jñānanetra as the avatārakanātha, the master who brought the Krama doctrine into the human domain. The recorded lineage begins with Netra or Jñānanetra, followed by Keyūravatī and Hrasvanātha.
He was also called Śivānanda

This alternative name appears consistently in modern scholarship and the surviving Krama lineage discussion.
Krama became an influential Śaiva tradition

The Krama was not an incidental local cult. Its doctrines were adopted, reworked or praised by major Kashmiri authors, including Abhinavagupta. Its literature continued for centuries and later appeared in forms preserved in Kerala and other regions.
Abhinavagupta inherited Krama material

Abhinavagupta discusses the sequence of Kālīs in the Tantrāloka, Tantrasāra, his own Kramastotra and other works. This does not make him Jñānanetra’s immediate disciple; it shows that Jñānanetra’s revelation lineage entered the intellectual system Abhinavagupta synthesized.
Early Krama teaching involved a sequence of Kālī forms

Scholarship connects the system with twelve or thirteen Kālīs whose functions articulate stages in manifestation, cognition and the consumption of time. Wenta argues that this developed from an earlier solar or Sun-Goddess sequence into the better-known doctrine of Kālīs as powers that consume time.
Grade B — strong traditional reconstruction
Jñānanetra was initiated in Uḍḍiyāna

Krama tradition locates his revelation at Uḍḍiyāna, often identified with the Swat region in present-day Pakistan. Oxford research on tantric geography notes that the Yonigahvaratantra connects his revelation with the Kāravīra cremation ground in Uḍḍiyāna.

This is a serious historical-geographical tradition, but it is not supported by a travel diary, inscription or contemporary external witness.
The yoginī Maṅgalā initiated him

Later accounts describe Jñānanetra meeting Maṅgalā, a yoginī or goddess, in the cremation ground and receiving the earliest Krama scriptures. This is central to lineage identity but belongs to revelation narrative rather than verifiable biography.
Three principal female disciples continued the lineage

Modern reconstructions name:

    Keyūravatī;

    Madanikā or Madanīkā;

    Kalyāṇikā.

The lineage’s female succession is supported by traditional lists, though their biographies and works are extremely thinly preserved. Claims about their precise number, roles and teachings should be attributed to the lineage sources rather than narrated as independent fact.
Grade C — hagiographic but philosophically meaningful

    yoginīs physically appeared in a cremation ground;

    they transmitted scripture through supernatural speech;

    Jñānanetra became fully awakened in a single encounter;

    the entire system entered human history at one moment;

    Kāravīra can be identified with one exact modern site;

    every teaching came unchanged from a divine circle;

    the lineage remained uninterrupted for a fixed number of generations;

    all principal disciples achieved identical realization.

These motifs legitimize the school by placing it outside ordinary human authorship.
Grade D — misleading claims to avoid
“Jñānanetra invented Kashmir Śaivism”

He was connected to one tradition within the larger Śaiva environment. Trika, Pratyabhijñā, Spanda and other currents have distinct textual and teacher histories.
“He founded the first feminist religion”

The presence of female gurus is historically striking, but modern political categories should not be projected onto the tradition without qualification.
“The Krama was a Kālī death cult”

Its imagery includes destruction, time and cremation-ground symbolism, but its philosophical aim concerns consciousness, perception and liberation.
“Uḍḍiyāna definitely means modern Swat”

Swat is a major scholarly identification, but tantric Uḍḍiyāna also became an idealized sacred geography. Treat the physical and visionary layers separately.
“The twelve Kālīs are twelve ordinary goddesses”

In Krama philosophy they often operate as stages or powers within cognition itself, not merely independent mythological personalities.
“The cremation-ground account proves occult events happened”

It proves that later lineage holders regarded the revelation as beyond ordinary scholastic authorship.
Uḍḍiyāna: the lost tantric homeland
The geographical identification

Uḍḍiyāna is widely associated with the Swat Valley and surrounding north-western regions of the Indian cultural world, now in Pakistan. The region was a major Buddhist centre, and later Tibetan accounts also associated it with Padmasambhava and tantric transmission.

The Krama tradition places Jñānanetra’s revelation there, possibly only a generation or two after the period traditionally associated with Padmasambhava. The overlap does not prove direct contact, but it shows that both Buddhist and Śaiva communities remembered the north-west as a source of highly charged tantric revelation.
The strong documentary question

    Why did competing tantric traditions claim that their most transformative teachings came from the same frontier region?

Possible explanations:

    Uḍḍiyāna was a genuine centre of ritual innovation.

    It lay outside dominant Brahmanical scholastic centres and thus accumulated an exotic reputation.

    Mobile Buddhist and Śaiva practitioners exchanged language, deity forms and ritual structures.

    Later authors used Uḍḍiyāna as a sacred origin point to authorize unconventional systems.

    Geography and visionary symbolism merged over time.

These possibilities can coexist.
