Kāravīra: the cremation ground

The Oxford discussion names the Kāravīra cremation ground as the place of revelation in the Yonigahvaratantra. It is also connected to a wider body of Uḍḍiyāna revelation narratives.
What a cremation ground means in tantra

Historically, cremation grounds were liminal places associated with:

    death;

    impurity;

    wandering ascetics;

    frightening deities;

    social marginality;

    impermanence;

    the collapse of ordinary identity;

    spirits and yoginīs in sacred imagination.

In the documentary, explain the symbolism without showing graphic material.

A safe visual vocabulary:

    extinguished ritual fires;

    smoke across an empty landscape;

    charred wood;

    ash carried by wind;

    dark riverbanks;

    circles of footprints;

    manuscript drawings;

    distant bells or percussion.

Deeper interpretation

The cremation ground is where all stable categories fail:

    body and object;

    purity and impurity;

    human and divine;

    fear and liberation;

    appearance and disappearance.

That makes it a fitting birthplace for a philosophy centred on how every experience emerges and is consumed.
The revelation from the yoginīs

Krama was sometimes described through expressions equivalent to teaching from the mouths of yoginīs. The claim means more than “women taught the system.”

It asserts that the tradition did not originate through:

    ordinary human reasoning;

    Vedic inheritance alone;

    institutional authorship;

    one male philosopher’s invention.

It arose from an encounter with forms of sacred feminine authority located outside conventional respectability.
Maṅgalā

The yoginī Maṅgalā is named in the traditional account as the being encountered by Jñānanetra at Kāravīra.

Her name means:

मङ्गला — maṅgalā
“Auspicious one.”

This creates a deliberate paradox:

    The supposedly terrifying cremation ground reveals the auspicious goddess.

That is a strong narrative motif. The place identified with death becomes the place where reality’s deepest vitality is revealed.
Keyūravatī: the nearly erased successor
Her place in the lineage

Traditional lists place Keyūravatī, also called Rājñī, immediately after Jñānanetra and before Hrasvanātha. Wenta cites the lineage as:

    Netra or Jñānanetra;

    Rājñī or Keyūravatī;

    Hrasvanātha or Vāmana.

राज्ञी — rājñī
“Queen.”

केयूरवती — keyūravatī
Approximately “she who possesses or wears an armlet,” depending on derivation.

Modern lineage presentations report that Jñānanetra’s three fully accomplished principal disciples were women, with Keyūravatī becoming his successor.
Why she is a strong follow-up subject

Almost no conventional biography survives.

We do not know securely:

    her family;

    birthplace;

    age;

    social identity;

    whether Keyūravatī was a personal name or initiatory title;

    what texts she composed;

    how she taught;

    where she lived;

    whether “Rājñī” meant queen literally or spiritually;

    how later historians decided which teachings were hers.

Yet a major lineage treated her as the link between divine revelation and its subsequent male authors.
Strong interpretive framing

    The tradition remembered that a woman carried the revelation, but did not preserve enough information to let her appear as a person.

This should not be narrated as proof that her history was deliberately suppressed. Manuscript loss, oral transmission, changing institutional priorities and the later fame of male commentators may all have contributed.
The Krama doctrine: consciousness as a sequence of Kālīs
Meaning of Krama

क्रम — krama

Possible meanings include:

    sequence;

    succession;

    ordered progression;

    step;

    process.

Krama is therefore not merely the school’s proper name. It expresses its central insight:

    Consciousness is recognized through the ordered sequence by which experience appears, stabilizes and dissolves.

The basic experiential sequence

A simplified non-initiatory model:

    An object appears.

    Attention reaches toward it.

    It is cognitively formed.

    It is enjoyed, rejected or interpreted.

    It passes away.

    Its absence is also known.

    Awareness remains as that which knew both appearance and disappearance.

Krama theology personifies or recognizes these dynamics as powers of Kālī.
Why Kālī?

Kālī is connected not only with destruction but with kāla, time.

Experience is temporal:

    it emerges;

    persists;

    changes;

    disappears.

But the Krama asks:

    What knows time passing?

The answer is not another object inside time. It is consciousness as the power that manifests and consumes temporal sequence.
The twelve Kālīs

Different sources and historical stages organize the Kālīs differently. Do not present one modern list as absolutely universal.

A commonly transmitted sequence includes names such as:

    Sṛṣṭikālī;

    Raktakālī;

    Sthitināśakālī;

    Yamakālī;

    Saṃhārakālī;

    Mṛtyukālī;

    Rudrakālī;

    Mārtaṇḍakālī;

    Paramārkakālī;

    Kālāgnirudrakālī;

    Mahākālakālī;

    Mahābhairavacaṇḍograghorakālī.

Spellings and enumeration vary.

Scholarship suggests the system evolved from a solar sequence—bhānavīkrama, the sequence of the Sun-Goddess—toward a doctrine emphasizing the consumption of time.
Better visual explanation

Show one ordinary perception:

A flame appears.

Then illustrate:

    initial emergence;

    colour and shape becoming defined;

    recognition as “flame”;

    emotional response;

    fading;

    memory;

    blankness;

    the awareness containing every phase.

Each stage is marked by a different ring, name or feminine silhouette.

This shows why the Krama is philosophically interesting instead of leaving viewers with twelve disconnected deities.
Jñānanetra’s surviving voice
Kālikāstotra

A short hymn to Kālī is attributed to Jñānanetra. Modern translations describe it as presenting Kālī not merely as a figure but as an all-pervading consciousness beyond simple categories of existence and nonexistence.

Because manuscript and editorial attribution questions remain, use:

    “A hymn transmitted under Jñānanetra’s name…”

rather than:

    “Jñānanetra certainly wrote this exact surviving text in the ninth century.”

Other connected texts

Texts or titles associated with early Krama transmission include:

    Yonigahvaratantra;

    Kramastotra;

    Khacakrastotra or hymn to the void/circle of space;

    Mahānayaprakāśa;

    Kramakeli, Abhinavagupta’s lost commentary;

    Mahārthamañjarī;

    different Kālī sequence texts and commentaries.

Not all were written by Jñānanetra. They represent different periods in the tradition’s development.
Lost works

Abhinavagupta’s Kramakeli is generally considered lost. It apparently dealt extensively with Krama doctrine. This is a powerful lost-book angle:

    The greatest Kashmiri philosopher may have written a major work explaining Jñānanetra’s lineage—but the work did not survive.

Fragments or quotations may remain inside later commentaries. A specialist interview could help identify what can be reconstructed.
