Recommended narrative structure
Act I — The system hidden behind Abhinavagupta

Begin with the familiar modern narrative:

    Vasugupta receives the Śivasūtras;

    Utpaladeva establishes Recognition philosophy;

    Abhinavagupta synthesizes the tradition.

Then reveal:

    Another lineage runs beneath this story—a Kālī tradition that Abhinavagupta considered powerful enough to absorb into his own system.

Introduce Jñānanetra.
Act II — Crossing into Uḍḍiyāna

Present the historical geography and competing interpretations.

Do not state the route as known. Show possible corridors and the political-religious environment of the north-west.

Explain Uḍḍiyāna’s role in both Buddhist and Śaiva memory.
Act III — The cremation-ground revelation

Tell the Maṅgalā narrative as lineage hagiography.

Key line:

    The first Krama scripture was not remembered as the work of a philosopher sitting safely in Kashmir, but as speech received where ordinary identity ended.

Use restrained visual symbolism.
Act IV — The Queen becomes the guru

Introduce Keyūravatī and the other women.

Reveal how little survives about them.

Pose the archival question:

    How can a woman be indispensable to a lineage and nearly absent from history?

Act V — What the twelve Kālīs actually mean

Move away from biography into a clear experiential model.

Use a perception unfolding in stages.

Make this the intellectual payoff:

    Kālī is not only the end of the world. She is the power by which this present thought emerges—and disappears before the next one.

Act VI — Abhinavagupta inherits the revelation

Explain:

    Krama influence;

    his discussion of the twelve Kālīs;

    his lost Kramakeli;

    integration into Trika;

    why the inheritor became more famous than the founder.

Act VII — The lineage travels south

Track the preservation of Mahānayaprakāśa material in Kerala.

This gives the story geographical closure:

    revelation in Uḍḍiyāna;

    systematization in Kashmir;

    manuscript survival in South India;

    global rediscovery through modern scholarship.

Ending

    Jñānanetra’s biography vanished, Keyūravatī’s voice was barely recorded, and Abhinavagupta’s great commentary on their system was lost.

    Yet the central claim survived: every object, every thought and every identity is already being consumed by the awareness in which it appears.

    The Krama was lost as an institution because its real temple was the sequence of experience itself.

