Opening hook

    Three centuries before Abhinavagupta wrote the Tantrāloka, an Indian court poet described a tantric master preparing a forbidden midnight ceremony outside the royal capital.

    The master was called Bhairavācārya.

    He asked a young warrior named Puṣyabhūti to stand guard while he completed a rite of Mahākāla near the cremation ground.

    Something rose against them. The king drew a supernatural sword. And when the battle ended, a goddess appeared and promised that his descendants would rule northern India.

    That descendant was Emperor Harṣa.

    But how much of this actually happened?

Why this subject matters

Bhairavācārya offers a rare window into early medieval tantra before it became internationally packaged as abstract “nondual philosophy.”

The story brings together:

    an early named tantric officiant;

    the Bhairava scriptures;

    cremation-ground religious culture;

    royal military patronage;

    spirit or vetāla-like beings;

    goddess legitimation of kingship;

    the historical city of Sthāneśvara;

    the dynasty of Emperor Harṣavardhana;

    literary fiction functioning as historical evidence;

    a possible bridge between Kāpālika ascetics and later Śaiva tantra.

The most useful larger thesis is:

    Tantra was not originally separate from politics. Its specialists offered rulers protection, weapons, supernatural legitimacy and access to divine power. Kings, in return, gave tantric lineages security, wealth and social reach.

The primary source: Bāṇa’s Harṣacarita

Bāṇabhaṭṭa composed the Harṣacarita in the seventh century as a literary biography of his patron, Emperor Harṣa. It is not a modern historical chronicle. It mixes courtly biography, ornate Sanskrit prose, political memory, myth and dynastic praise.

The Bhairavācārya episode appears in the third ucchvāsa, or chapter, during the story of Harṣa’s supposed ancestor Puṣyabhūti.
