The Hindi/Sanskrit educational material explicitly summarizes that Bhairavācārya presented Puṣyabhūti with the extraordinary sword Aṭṭahāsa and requested his assistance in obtaining ritual accomplishment.
What the story says

The full episode should be checked directly against the Sanskrit and the Cowell–Thomas translation before scripting exact details. The broad narrative is:

    Puṣyabhūti encounters an impressive Śaiva ascetic named Bhairavācārya.

    The ascetic possesses signs of learning, initiation and supernatural reputation.

    A bond develops between the ruler and the adept.

    Bhairavācārya gives or reveals the sword called Aṭṭahāsa, “Loud Laughter.”

    The ascetic requests royal protection during the completion of a powerful nocturnal rite.

    The rite is associated with Mahākāla and takes place in a cemetery or cremation-ground environment.

    Puṣyabhūti and companions stand guard.

    A hostile supernatural figure or obstructing being appears.

    Puṣyabhūti confronts the threat.

    The Goddess manifests and grants him dynastic sovereignty.

    The Pushyabhūti line is thereby represented as divinely authorized.

The account should be narrated as:

    “Bāṇa tells us…”

rather than:

    “This is what historically happened.”

Evidence grading
Grade A: strong historical context
Bāṇa wrote the episode in the seventh century

The Harṣacarita is a near-contemporary work written at Harṣa’s court. Although the ancestor story is mythologized, it gives important evidence for what a sophisticated seventh-century Sanskrit audience considered a recognizable Śaiva tantric practitioner.
Sthāneśvara was a major Śaiva royal centre

Modern Thanesar in Haryana was connected with Harṣa’s dynasty and was deeply associated with Śiva under the name Sthāṇu or Sthāneśvara. The region’s religious landscape included temples, sacred water and cremation-ground associations.
Early Śaiva specialists operated around cremation grounds

Bakker’s comparison between the Harṣacarita and the Skandapurāṇa concludes that the Mahākapāla area around Sthāneśvara was presented as a gathering place for accomplished Śaiva ascetics, including Pāśupata and Kāpālika-type siddhas.
Bhairavācārya’s ritual resembles Bhairava-tantric material

Academic work identifies his cultic context with the Bhairavatantras. The scene contains ritual vocabulary and features consistent with early Śaiva tantric literature rather than being an entirely generic ghost story.
Royal patronage and tantric specialists were historically connected

Early medieval rulers across South Asia supported Śaiva officiants who performed initiations, protection rites, temple ceremonies and rituals of victory or sovereignty. The precise event in Bāṇa is literary, but the social relationship is historically credible.
Grade B: historically plausible, not independently verified
A real Bhairavācārya may lie behind the character

Bāṇa provides enough specialized detail that scholars take the portrait seriously as evidence for contemporary tantric culture. However, no securely identified inscription or independent biography currently confirms this particular master.

The name may be:

    a personal initiatory title;

    a lineage title;

    “teacher of Bhairava”;

    a literary name chosen to make his religious affiliation obvious.

The rite called Mahākālahṛdaya may reflect a real ritual system

Gargi Sharma describes the episode as an occult practice named mahākālahṛdaya, whose preparation culminates in a cemetery rite. The details suggest knowledge of an existing ritual vocabulary, but the surviving literary description may dramatize or modify the ceremony.
The royal ancestor may preserve historical patronage memory

Harṣa’s family probably had genuine relationships with Śaiva institutions. The Puṣyabhūti story could transform that historical patronage into a miraculous foundation legend.
Grade C: dynastic legend and sacred narrative

    the sword Aṭṭahāsa possessed supernatural power;

    a hostile spirit physically emerged;

    Puṣyabhūti defeated it exactly as narrated;

    the Goddess manifested visibly;

    she accurately predicted the future empire;

    Bhairavācārya achieved a supernatural siddhi that night;

    Harṣa’s dynasty was founded through one ceremony.

These details explain royal identity and sacred authority. They should not be treated as independently confirmed events.
Grade D: claims to avoid
“This is the first tantric ritual in Indian history”

There are older ritual and textual precedents. It is better described as one of the earliest rich literary portraits of an identifiable Śaiva tantric ceremony and officiant.
“Bhairavācārya was definitely a Kāpālika”

The episode shares features with Kāpālika and Bhairava-oriented culture, but Bāṇa’s terminology and the historical categories should be handled carefully. Say “Kāpālika-associated” or “Bhairava-tantric” unless quoting a scholar’s specific classification.
“He summoned a vampire”

The Sanskrit concept of a vetāla does not map neatly onto the modern European vampire. Depending on context it can mean a spirit associated with bodies, cemeteries and possession.
“The Pushyabhūti dynasty literally began through black magic”

The ritual is a sacred dynasty-origin story inside court literature.
“This was Kashmir Śaivism”

The event predates Abhinavagupta and belongs to a broader early Śaiva tantric world. Later Kashmir traditions inherited related Bhairava scriptures and concepts, but direct institutional continuity is not established.
“The rite can be reconstructed and repeated”

The video should remain historical and textual. Do not give procedural details, mantras, quantities, ritual sequences or practical instructions for cemetery ceremonies.
Who was Bhairavācārya?
The name

भैरवाचार्य — bhairavācārya

    Bhairava: a fierce form or supreme manifestation of Śiva in several tantric traditions.

    Ācārya: authorized teacher, officiant or preceptor.

The title can therefore mean:

    “The teacher of the Bhairava system.”

This may be more like an institutional or initiatory identity than a birth name.
His appearance and identity

Bāṇa’s portrait should be extracted directly from chapter three and compared with academic interpretation. It reportedly presents a visibly marked ascetic whose clothing, objects, assistants and manner would signal a Śaiva tantric specialist to the original audience.

The visual value is high because the episode allows reconstruction of:

    the ascetic’s ritual dress;

    his relationship with attendants;

    an early Śaiva sacred landscape;

    a king serving as guardian rather than master;

    the tension between respectable court and cremation-ground religion.

Avoid making him look like a modern orange-robed yoga teacher. The historical world is much closer to early medieval ascetic and Kāpālika iconography.
Mahākāla and the “heart” ritual
The term

महाकालहृदय — mahākālahṛdaya

Possible literal rendering:

    “The Heart of Mahākāla.”

In tantric terminology, hṛdaya, “heart,” can signify:

    an essence;

    a central mantra or formula;

    the innermost identity of a deity;

    the core of a scripture or ritual system.

Do not translate it simply as “heart spell” without explanation.
Mahākāla

Mahākāla means “Great Time” or “Great Death,” depending on interpretive emphasis.

In early Śaiva religion he can represent Śiva as the force that:

    consumes all beings;

    rules time and death;

    inhabits or transcends the cremation ground;

    grants protection and sovereignty;

    turns terror into divine power.

This gives the episode a political logic:

    A king who symbolically confronts death under Mahākāla’s authority becomes fit to rule beings who are themselves subject to time and death.

Why a ruler was needed

The literary rite reverses the expected hierarchy.

The king is not there to command the ascetic. He stands watch while the ascetic conducts the ceremony. Political power protects ritual power; ritual power then legitimizes political power.

That reciprocal arrangement is the key historical insight.
The sword Aṭṭahāsa
Meaning

अट्टहास — aṭṭahāsa

“Roaring laughter,” “loud laughter” or “terrible laughter.”

Aṭṭahāsa is a powerful name because fierce Śaiva and goddess imagery often associates divine laughter with:

    the destruction of enemies;

    the shattering of ordinary reality;

    terrifying freedom;

    triumph over death;

    the eruption of supernatural power.

Narrative role

The sword acts as:

    a gift from tantric authority;

    a mark of royal chosenness;

    a weapon against forces obstructing the rite;

    a symbolic transfer of Bhairava’s violence to the king;

    an object connecting ascetic and warrior power.

Strong visual motif

Build the entire thumbnail and video around an illustrated sword inscription:

AṬṬAHĀSA
THE SWORD THAT LAUGHED

Do not claim the physical sword has been discovered. No securely identified surviving artifact is known from the material currently located.
The being in the cemetery

Popular Hindi summaries often call the scene a vetāla sādhana, but the exact Sanskrit terminology and sequence must be checked in the primary passage.
Responsible description

Use:

    “A hostile cemetery-being or supernatural obstacle appears within Bāṇa’s account.”

Then explain possible categories:

    vetāla;

    spirit associated with a body;

    guardian or obstacle;

    embodiment of ritual danger;

    literary test of royal courage.

Avoid giving the being a definitive species where the source is ambiguous.
Why such a being appears

In tantric sacred biography, an obstructing being can represent:

    the danger surrounding the ritual;

    hostile powers being compelled;

    the practitioner’s fear;

    competing supernatural authority;

    the boundary between human and nonhuman worlds;

    the king’s fitness to receive sovereignty.

The supernatural battle is therefore also a political initiation.
The goddess and the creation of sovereignty

The climax is not Bhairavācārya’s private power. It is the Goddess’s recognition of Puṣyabhūti.

She effectively transforms:

    a warrior into a divinely chosen king;

    a ritual guardian into a dynasty-founder;

    local authority into future imperial sovereignty.

Documentary argument

Royal genealogies often place supernatural authorization at their beginning because ordinary conquest alone does not explain why one family has the right to rule.

The story gives Harṣa’s family three types of legitimacy:

    Ascetic legitimacy — recognition by a powerful master.

    Martial legitimacy — courage during supernatural danger.

    Goddess legitimacy — explicit divine promise of sovereignty.

