Recommended narrative structure
Act I — Tantra before the philosophy books

Begin with Abhinavagupta and familiar Kashmir Śaivism imagery.

Then move backward three centuries:

    Before consciousness became the elegant language of nondual philosophy, Bhairava’s teachers appeared in royal literature as dangerous ascetics operating beside cremation grounds.

Introduce Bāṇa and Harṣa.
Act II — A king meets Bhairavācārya

Describe the ascetic from the primary source.

Establish the reciprocal fascination:

    the king possesses force;

    the ascetic possesses knowledge;

    neither can complete the event alone.

Act III — The sword that laughs

Introduce Aṭṭahāsa.

Use the sword as the narrative object linking the two worlds.
Act IV — Midnight at Mahākapāla

Map Sthāneśvara.

Explain the real religious landscape identified by Bakker:

    cremation ground;

    tank;

    Śaiva temples;

    ascetic settlement;

    royal capital nearby.

Then carefully retell the ceremony without practical instructions.
Act V — The king confronts the obstacle

Present the supernatural event as Bāṇa’s narrative.

Offer three interpretations:

    literal sacred history;

    dramatized memory of a dangerous ritual;

    allegory of royal courage overcoming chaos.

Act VI — The Goddess creates a dynasty

Explain divine kingship.

The rite does not merely benefit Bhairavācārya: it produces the sacred origin of Harṣa’s family.
Act VII — What historians can salvage

Separate the layers onscreen:
Layer	Confidence
Bāṇa wrote the account	High
Bhairava-oriented ascetics existed near royal centres	High
Sthāneśvara contained a major Śaiva landscape	High
A real teacher inspired Bhairavācārya	Plausible
Puṣyabhūti guarded this exact rite	Uncertain
A supernatural being appeared	Sacred narrative
A goddess promised the empire	Dynastic hagiography
Ending

    Bhairavācārya may never have summoned a spirit, and the Goddess may never have spoken beside a physical fire.

    But Bāṇa’s audience believed that the most dangerous religious power in their world lived just outside the city—and that kings ruled safely only when they learned how to stand beside it.

