Research Pack 05: Jamana Gubhaju — Kathmandu’s Tantric Who Entered the City Beneath the City
Core video thesis

Jamana Gubhaju is one of Kathmandu’s most famous forgotten figures: a seventeenth-century Newar Buddhist priest remembered as a healer, rain-maker, exorcist and master of subterranean Kathmandu.

His legends connect:

    a sealed ritual chamber at Swayambhunath;

    a well allegedly leading into underground passages;

    King Pratap Malla and a catastrophic drought;

    a sacred painting connected with serpent deities;

    contests between local and foreign magicians;

    the household Vajrayāna priesthood of the Newars;

    ritual institutions and physical locations that still survive.

Yet almost everything available in English collapses legend, local memory and history into one seamless biography.

The strongest documentary is therefore not:

    “A Nepalese wizard really travelled through secret tunnels.”

It is:

    How did one Newar priest become the supernatural guardian of Kathmandu—and what historical world is hidden inside his legends?

Jamana appears to have been remembered under the personal or initiatory name Mañju Vajra Bajracharya. Regional sources usually place him around the seventeenth century and connect him with Musum Bahal in Kathmandu, although firm contemporary documentation remains much thinner than the later stories suggest.
