Recommended documentary structure
Act I — The door no tourist enters

Begin at Shantipur.

Show the enormous visibility of Swayambhunath and the obscurity of the small closed shrine.

Introduce the drought legend.
Act II — Who was a gubhāju?

Move into Newar Buddhism.

Explain why a married ritual priest could simultaneously be:

    Buddhist;

    hereditary;

    tantric;

    a healer;

    a civic protector.

This prevents the entire episode becoming “Nepalese Harry Potter.”
Act III — The historical Jamana disappears

Present the limited evidence:

    Mañju Vajra;

    Malla-era dating;

    Musum Bahal;

    four siddha priests;

    uncertain manuscripts.

Show a biographical form filling with blank spaces.
Act IV — The city beneath the city

Map:

    the well;

    Musum Bahal;

    Kankeshwari;

    palace;

    Swayambhunath;

    Shantipur.

Separate surveyed geography from legendary routes.
Act V — Drought

Tell the Pratap Malla episode.

Use three versions of the story side by side to show how oral traditions mutate.
Act VI — The magician from India

Tell one restrained version of the contest.

Interpret it as a claim about Kathmandu’s ritual sovereignty.
Act VII — What “magic” meant

Explain that healing, rain rites, spirit pacification and urban protection were not necessarily separate categories.

The priest maintained relations between visible and invisible society.
Act VIII — Why Jamana survived

Show:

    book;

    comic;

    film;

    local ritual;

    heritage sites;

    online storytelling.

He survived because he became a model of Kathmandu itself: hidden, layered, dangerous, compassionate and impossible to reduce to one religion.
Ending

    Whether or not a tunnel ever reached from Musum Bahal to Swayambhunath, Jamana’s stories reveal a real underground city—the network of priests, wells, courtyards, deities and neighbourhood obligations beneath the official history of Kathmandu.

    He did not merely live in that city. In legend, he was the only person who knew how all its hidden parts connected.

