Research Pack 06: Gopīchand and Maynamatī — The Queen Who Forced Her Son to Abandon a Kingdom
Core video thesis

Across Bengal, Bangladesh, Rajasthan, Punjab, Gujarat and the Hindi-speaking north, singers preserved the story of a king named Gopīchand who was compelled by his mother Maynamatī to surrender his throne and become a Nāth yogi.

Yet the regional versions disagree about almost everything:

    whether he was called Gopīchand, Gopīchandra or Govindacandra;

    whether his teacher was Jālandharnāth, Hāḍipā or Hājipā;

    whether Maynamatī was a historical Chandra-dynasty queen;

    whether Gopīchand returned after twelve years;

    whether he betrayed and buried his guru;

    whether his final renunciation was voluntary;

    whether the story began in Buddhist Bengal or within an already Śaiva Nāth environment.

The most compelling documentary thesis is:

    Gopīchand may preserve the memory of a historical eastern king, but his legend became something larger: a portable argument over whether family, kingship, masculinity and even spiritual powers must be abandoned for liberation.

Unlike most renunciation legends, the central religious authority is not initially the male guru. It is Gopīchand’s mother.
