Thumbnail text:

LEAVE YOUR KINGDOM

Alternative:

BURY THE GURU?

The second title is highly clickable, but it should not imply that the episode is established history.
Opening hook

    A king sits inside his palace surrounded by wives, servants and wealth.

    His mother enters and tells him that everything he owns will disappear.

    She does not ask him to rule justly. She orders him to abandon the throne, submit to a low-status wandering teacher and disappear for twelve years.

    He obeys—but when he returns with supernatural powers, pride takes control.

    At the urging of his wives, the king turns against his own guru and has him buried beneath the earth.

    This story was sung from Bengal to Rajasthan. But no two regions remembered the same king.

Why this subject is exceptional

Gopīchand combines several strong content engines.
1. One legend across multiple civilizations

Versions survive in:

    Bengali;

    Assamese and eastern regional traditions;

    Hindi;

    Rajasthani;

    Punjabi;

    Gujarati;

    Sindhi-related oral environments;

    Nepali Nāth reception.

The story therefore offers a rare opportunity to watch one yogic narrative mutate as it crosses languages and social worlds.
2. A woman controls the renunciation narrative

Maynamatī is not merely a grieving mother left behind by an enlightened son. She:

    already understands impermanence;

    recognizes the guru;

    pushes Gopīchand toward initiation;

    resists palace interests;

    interprets kingship as spiritually dangerous;

    sometimes possesses powers or superior knowledge herself.

3. Spiritual power becomes a trap

After his period of asceticism, Gopīchand reportedly returns and demonstrates siddhis to his wives. Rather than proving liberation, the display marks regression. Banglapedia’s account says that his guru rebukes him, after which Gopīchand becomes angry and has the guru buried; another disciple rescues the teacher, and Gopīchand ultimately repents and renounces permanently.
4. History and folklore genuinely intersect

There were Chandra rulers in eastern Bengal, and the archaeological region of Mainamati in present-day Bangladesh preserves major Buddhist remains. The legend’s names overlap suggestively with this historical setting, but the correspondence is not clean enough to identify the hero confidently with one documented king.
5. The story remains performative

Rajasthani Nāth singers still preserve long oral versions. Ann Grodzins Gold’s A Carnival of Parting centres on performances by Madhu Natisar Nath and shows that Gopīchand’s story remained part of hereditary oral Nāth culture rather than surviving only in manuscripts.
Who was Gopīchand?
Names

Common forms include:

    Gopīchand

    Gopīcandra

    Gopīchandra

    Govindacandra

    Govicandra

    regional pronunciations such as Gopīcand or Gopicand

The variation is not automatically evidence for separate people. Oral transmission, scribal changes and adaptation across languages frequently alter royal and yogic names.

However, Govindacandra is also a historically common royal name. It should not be assumed that every historical king bearing it is the Nāth hero.
Sanskrit form

गोपीचन्द्र — gopīcandra

The name can be read approximately as “moon of the cowherd women,” though historical names do not always function as deliberate theological compounds.
The basic legendary identity

Most versions remember him as:

    a powerful eastern king;

    son of Maynamatī;

    nephew or relative of Bhartr̥hari in some northern traditions;

    disciple of Jālandharnāth or Hāḍipā;

    one of the Nine Nāths in some lists;

    a reluctant renouncer;

    a yogi whose initial attainment remained compromised by pride.

A Ravi Varma Press chromolithograph identifies “Yogi Gopichandra” as a royal figure who becomes the disciple of Jalandhara Nātha at Maynamatī’s insistence. This represents the popular printed tradition, not independent historical proof.

