Evidence grading
Grade A — strongly documented literary and oral tradition
Gopīchand is a major Nāth legendary king

Gopīchand appears across extensive regional oral and literary traditions as a king who leaves royal life and becomes a Nāth yogi. He is frequently paired with Bhartr̥hari, another royal renouncer.
Bengali Nāth literature preserves a Maynamatī–Gopīchand cycle

Banglapedia identifies Manikchandra’s Song, Maynamatī’s Song and Gopīchandra’s Song as related versions of the same narrative complex. It describes Gopīchand’s discipleship under Hāḍipā, twelve-year ascetic period, misuse of powers, rebellion against his guru and final repentance.
The tradition was preserved by named Bengali poets and collectors

Banglapedia associates versions with Durlabh Mallik, Bhavānī Dās and Śukur Mahmud, whose Gopīchānder Sannyās became an important example of medieval Bengali Nāth literature. Collectors and editors preserved multiple manuscripts and oral forms during the nineteenth and twentieth centuries.
Long Rajasthani oral performances survive

The University of California’s open edition of A Carnival of Parting presents Gopīchand and Bhartr̥hari narratives as performed by the Rajasthani Nāth singer Madhu Natisar Nath. This is one of the best English-language sources for examining performance, translation and living oral variation.

