The legend became a major popular-print subject

The Ravi Varma Press produced chromolithographs depicting Gopīchand’s royal and renunciatory life, showing that the story entered mass visual culture rather than remaining only an oral ascetic tradition.
Grade B — historically plausible elements
The story may preserve memories of the Chandra kingdom of eastern Bengal

The Mainamati area near Comilla contains dozens of Buddhist archaeological sites associated with the ancient region of Samataṭa. Later tradition connects its name with a queen Maynamatī and a ruler Govindacandra. The overlap with the Nāth legend is significant but does not establish a one-to-one historical identity.
Maynamatī could preserve the memory of a royal woman

Royal women frequently acted as patrons and religious intermediaries in early medieval South Asia. A historical queen may stand behind the name, but the yogic master who orders her son’s renunciation is primarily preserved through later narrative literature.
Eastern Buddhist, Śaiva and Nāth elements may have blended

Older histories of Bengali literature described the region’s Nāth religion as combining Buddhist and Śaiva elements. Modern scholarship is more cautious about simple “mixture” theories, but Bengal’s post-Pāla religious world clearly provided opportunities for shared vocabulary, teachers and practices to be reclassified across traditions.
The teacher Hāḍipā may connect to Jālandharipa

Regional versions identify Gopīchand’s guru as:

    Hāḍipā;

    Hājipā;

    Hāḍināth;

    Jālandharnāth;

    Jālandharipa.

These may reflect a historical process in which the Buddhist mahāsiddha Jālandharipa and the Nāth Jālandharnāth became identified, localized or transformed.

This is plausible, but each manuscript must be checked rather than assuming all names originally denoted one teacher.
Grade C — hagiographic and folkloric episodes

    Maynamatī could foresee her son’s death.

    Gopīchand would die unless initiated for exactly twelve years.

    The king passed through impossible ordeals.

    He learned supernatural transformations.

    His wives persuaded him to attack or bury his guru.

    The guru survived underground through yogic power.

    Kāṇha or another disciple detected the burial supernaturally.

    Gopīchand travelled between distant kingdoms by miraculous means.

    His body became immortal.

    Every regional version descends unchanged from one medieval original.

These stories are invaluable for interpreting Nāth values, but they are not modern biography.
Grade D — claims to avoid
“Gopīchand was definitely King Govindacandra of the Chandra dynasty”

The names and setting overlap, but chronology and genealogy vary too widely.
“Maynamatī founded the Mainamati archaeological monasteries”

The archaeological sites predate or span multiple political phases. The modern place-name and legendary queen should not be used to assign individual foundations without inscriptional evidence.
“Gopīchand was originally Buddhist and later stolen by Hindus”

Buddhist siddha, Śaiva yogic and Nāth identities developed through overlapping environments. “Stolen” imposes a modern ownership dispute on fluid medieval traditions.
“He was literally buried alive as a yogic feat”

In the legend, the buried figure is usually the guru, and the story is not an instruction or reliable record of a practical feat.
“Maynamatī hated her son’s wives”

Her opposition represents a clash between dynastic continuation and renunciation. Reducing it to domestic jealousy removes the religious argument.
“The tale proves that family life prevents enlightenment”

The narrative reflects ascetic Nāth values, while historical Nāth communities also included householders. Modern scholarship distinguishes householder Nath groups from ascetic branches.
The central conflict: a mother against the kingdom

In many renunciation narratives, the family begs the saint to remain.

Gopīchand’s legend reverses this.

Maynamatī understands that:

    royal life is impermanent;

    beauty and power decay;

    kingship multiplies attachment;

    palace pleasures conceal death;

    only initiation can alter her son’s fate.

The court, especially Gopīchand’s wives, sees the situation differently:

    a king has obligations;

    the dynasty requires continuity;

    renunciation abandons subjects;

    the mother’s spiritual authority threatens the palace;

    the guru appears socially inferior and politically disruptive.

Strong documentary question

    Is Maynamatī saving her son—or destroying the lives of everyone dependent upon him?

The legend rarely gives the wives the same spiritual authority as the mother, but their resistance is rational from within the political world.
Maynamatī as female guru
What makes her unusual

Maynamatī often knows more than the king and recognizes the guru before her son does. Depending on the version, she:

    possesses esoteric knowledge;

    has undergone instruction herself;

    sees impending death;

    arranges the initiation;

    teaches impermanence;

    defeats Gopīchand rhetorically;

    resists emotional manipulation;

    places liberation above royal succession.

She can therefore be presented as a mediating guru, even when the formal initiation belongs to Hāḍipā or Jālandharnāth.
Better framing

    Maynamatī does not initiate Gopīchand herself. She creates the conditions under which initiation becomes unavoidable.

Gender tension

The tale simultaneously empowers and limits her:

    she governs the spiritual decision;

    but authority ultimately passes to a male guru;

    her knowledge is justified through maternal concern;

    later performances may portray her as saint, witch, strategist or cruel mother.

Comparing versions would show how regional audiences negotiated female religious authority.
The twelve-year renunciation

Banglapedia’s summary states that Gopīchand becomes Hāḍipā’s disciple and lives as an ascetic for twelve years before returning.
Why twelve years?

Twelve years often functions in Indian religious narrative as:

    a full cycle of discipline;

    the duration of a major vow;

    proof of separation from ordinary identity;

    a symbolic completion period;

    a unit long enough to transform a king beyond recognition.

Do not claim one universal Nāth rule requiring twelve years unless a specific source states it.
Narrative function

The period removes:

    royal clothing;

    control over servants;

    sexual privilege;

    public identity;

    inherited status.

Gopīchand must become dependent on the guru he initially considers beneath him.
The guru’s humiliation of the king

Regional tales often make Gopīchand undergo deliberately humiliating tests.

These may include being required to:

    beg at his former palace;

    address his wives or mother from the position of a mendicant;

    accept food from those who once served him;

    surrender signs of masculinity and kingship;

    obey a guru associated with a marginal social group.

Exact trials differ and should be sourced to the version being narrated.
Why humiliation matters

The guru does not merely teach information.

He dismantles:

    royal reflexes;

    class superiority;

    possessiveness;

    the expectation of obedience;

    the king’s identity as the centre of the world.

This is the deeper logic behind the harsh episodes.
The buried guru

Banglapedia gives the compressed narrative:

    Gopīchand returns after his ascetic period.

    He displays magical abilities to entertain his wives.

    Hāḍipā rebukes him.

    Gopīchand becomes angry.

    At the wives’ urging, he buries his guru.

    Kāṇupā rescues Hāḍipā.

    Gopīchand repents and renounces permanently.

Why this is the film’s strongest dark turn

The event reverses the initiation:

    first the guru symbolically destroys the king;

    later the king attempts to remove the guru physically;

    the palace reclaims the disciple through pride;

    siddhis become instruments of regression;

    the disciple’s apparent success reveals spiritual failure.

Strong thesis line

    Gopīchand’s first renunciation is imposed by his mother. His second begins only after he understands what he has done.

Three interpretations
Moral interpretation

Spiritual powers without humility corrupt the practitioner.
Social interpretation

The king cannot occupy both palace hierarchy and guru-disciple submission.
Psychological interpretation

The buried guru represents the disciple’s attempt to suppress the authority that exposed his false identity.

Do not present the third as the traditional meaning; identify it as a modern interpretive reading.
Hāḍipā and caste ambiguity

The name Hāḍipā is sometimes associated in Bengali interpretation with a socially marginal or occupational identity. This creates an important narrative inversion:

    the king must submit to someone socially beneath him;

    the mother recognizes spiritual authority that the palace rejects;

    caste or occupational contempt becomes an obstacle to realization.

However, the exact etymology and social identity vary by source. Do not confidently describe Hāḍipā as belonging to a specific caste without verifying the Bengali manuscript and commentary being used.
Research question

Was the guru’s low status:

    part of the earliest tale;

    a Bengali localization of Jālandharipa;

    an intentional challenge to Brahmanical hierarchy;

    a later folk reinterpretation of an unfamiliar siddha name?

This could become a major chapter in the documentary.
Historical Bengal and the Chandra question
Mainamati archaeology

Mainamati, near Comilla in Bangladesh, contains more than fifty Buddhist archaeological sites dating broadly from the early medieval period. The landscape belonged to the historic Samataṭa region and preserves evidence of a major Buddhist monastic culture.

