Historical possibilities

The legend could preserve:

    a distorted memory of a Chandra ruler;

    a royal name attached retrospectively to an older Nāth tale;

    a Buddhist regional narrative absorbed into Nāth literature;

    a fully legendary king localized at a real royal site;

    several kings fused into one hero.

What would count as stronger evidence?

    a dated inscription naming Maynamatī;

    a genealogy matching the legend;

    an early manuscript linking Gopīchand to Samataṭa;

    consistent chronology between the Chandra dynasty and Jālandharipa;

    archaeological evidence of Nāth activity at Mainamati;

    early non-literary references to a renouncing king.

At present, the evidence supports an intriguing connection, not a solved identification.
The story’s migration across India
Bengal and Bangladesh

The eastern cycle emphasizes:

    Maynamatī;

    King Manikcandra;

    Govindacandra/Gopīchandra;

    Hāḍipā;

    palace women;

    guru betrayal;

    regional social detail.

