Recommended documentary structure
Act I — A mother orders a king to leave

Begin inside the palace.

Introduce Maynamatī’s warning and Gopīchand’s resistance.

Do not immediately explain whether he was historical.
Act II — The king with too many biographies

Place versions on a map:

    Bangladesh;

    Bengal;

    Rajasthan;

    Punjab;

    Gujarat;

    Maharashtra;

    Nepal.

Each region supplies a different relative, guru, trial or ending.
Act III — Searching for the historical kingdom

Move to Mainamati and the Chandra rulers.

Establish:

    real archaeology;

    real royal names;

    absence of decisive proof;

    how historical places attract legendary biographies.

Act IV — The queen who knew first

Focus on Maynamatī.

Explore:

    maternal authority;

    female esoteric knowledge;

    conflict with the wives;

    tension between dynasty and liberation.

Act V — A king becomes the lowest disciple

Explain guru submission and social inversion.

The palace ruler must beg, obey and accept humiliation.
Act VI — Twelve years later

Gopīchand returns with powers.

For a moment, the audience expects triumph.

Then reveal that the display of siddhis is evidence of failure.
Act VII — The buried guru

Tell the episode carefully as regional sacred narrative.

Emphasize the reversal of trust and authority.
Act VIII — The second renunciation

Gopīchand repents.

The first renunciation belonged to Maynamatī’s command; the final one belongs to his own recognition.
Act IX — The king becomes a song

Show the narrative leaving Bengal:

    singers;

    print;

    theatre;

    chromolithographs;

    cassette and YouTube recordings.

Ending

    A historical king may be hidden somewhere inside Gopīchand’s story. But the man remembered across India was more useful than any king could be.

    He was the ruler who proved that wealth could not save him, the yogi who proved that powers could not perfect him, and the disciple who had to betray his teacher before understanding what renunciation meant.

