Misleading framings to avoid
“A mother destroyed her son’s life”

The legend regards Maynamatī as seeing a danger that the palace cannot perceive. Present the emotional cost without dismissing her authority.
“His wives were evil temptresses”

They represent dynastic life, emotional attachment and political responsibility. Some versions may demonize them, but the documentary need not.
“The guru was an untouchable who proved caste does not matter”

The social reading is important, but the guru’s exact identity must be established from each source.
“The story is a factual biography of a Chandra king”

Treat the historical identification as a hypothesis.
“Buddhism became Hinduism”

The transition involved multiple regional processes, not one clean replacement.
“Siddhis made Gopīchand enlightened”

The story’s central moral is almost the opposite.
“Nāths always rejected marriage and family”

Nāth traditions included both renunciants and householders.
