Research Pack 07: Vāk Vajra — The Newar Master Who Carried a Stupa From Varanasi to Kathmandu
Core video thesis

Kathmandu tradition remembers Vāk Vajra as a married Buddhist tantric master who travelled miraculously to Varanasi, humiliated a sceptical king, lifted an entire stupa from its foundations, carried it across the Himalayas, and finally entered a sealed meditation room from which he never returned.

A real historical complex survives behind the legend:

    Maitrīpur Mahāvihāra, or Kwā Bāhā;

    Śrīghaḥ/Kāśī Swayambhu, a major Kathmandu stupa;

    a Vajrācārya family and ritual community claiming descent from Vāk Vajra;

    inscriptions and manuscripts attached to the relevant monasteries;

    a city-wide tradition of four siddha Vajrācāryas associated with Kathmandu’s territorial divisions.

But his possible dates range from the fifth to the sixteenth century—a difference of roughly one thousand years. A 2025 academic study acknowledges the contradictions and concludes only very broadly that he probably lived between the twelfth and sixteenth centuries, while several traditions place him much earlier.

The strongest documentary question is therefore:

    Did the legend preserve one forgotten master—or did Kathmandu gather centuries of Buddhist architecture, manuscripts and local memory around a single supernatural name?

