Recommended documentary structure
Act I — A stupa with two cities inside it

Begin at Śrīghaḥ.

Ask why a Kathmandu monument is called Kāśī Swayambhu.

Tell the miracle in thirty seconds, then pull back:

    The monument can be dated. The man cannot.

Act II — The priest who belonged to a household

Explain Newar Vajrācāryas:

    married;

    hereditary;

    monastically affiliated;

    ritually specialized;

    embedded in city neighbourhoods.

This prevents viewers from imagining Vāk Vajra as a Tibetan monk or Hindu ascetic.
Act III — Four possible centuries

Put the timelines onscreen.

Each proposed birth date moves him into a completely different Nepal:

    Licchavi;

    early medieval tantric;

    post-Indian Buddhist;

    Malla urban world.

Ask what evidence each chronology requires.
Act IV — The king doubts the consecration

Tell the Varanasi episode.

Focus on the deceptive simplicity of ritual authority.

The king believes he has witnessed almost nothing; the master believes the entire stupa has already been transformed.
Act V — The stupa flies

Present the miracle through animation or traditional visual language.

Then separate:

    real stupa;

    inscriptional evidence;

    oral legend;

    unresolved Indian origin.

Act VI — Sacred geography moves north

Interpret the relocation.

Kathmandu does not merely imitate Varanasi. It absorbs and reproduces it.
Act VII — The room that was never opened

Return to Kwā Bāhā.

Tell the eternal-meditation tradition.

Avoid a cheap “what is inside?” reveal. The real point is why the lineage refuses to convert its founder into a dead ancestor.
Act VIII — One man or many?

Present the accumulated evidence:

    different centuries;

    multiple monuments;

    textual attributions;

    descendants;

    four-city-zone scheme.

Possible conclusion:

    Vāk Vajra may be best understood as both a historical teacher and a lineage persona larger than any one biography.

Ending

    The stupa probably never flew through the sky, and Vāk Vajra’s body may not be sitting unchanged behind a sealed door.

    But the legend records something historically real: Kathmandu’s Buddhist priests did not see themselves as guardians of a fading Indian tradition. They saw their city as a sacred centre capable of moving Kāśī itself.

