Research Pack 08: Lakulīśa — The Dead Brahmin Whose Body Śiva Entered
Core video thesis

One of India’s oldest organized Śaiva ascetic traditions traced its human lineage to a disturbing miracle: Śiva descended at Kāyāvarohaṇa, entered the abandoned body of a dead Brahmin, rose carrying a wooden club and taught a path in which seekers deliberately behaved like madmen to convert public humiliation into spiritual power.

The resulting Pāśupata movement was not a tiny local cult. By the fourth century CE it possessed documented teacher lineages and institutions at Mathura; its images spread from Gujarat and Rajasthan to Odisha, Bengal and the Deccan; and later Śaiva systems inherited its model of initiation, guru succession and liberation through union with Śiva. The difficulty is that the earliest Pāśupata scripture does not call its revealer Lakulīśa. The named biography appears more clearly in later sources, raising the possibility that a historical teacher, a divine incarnation and an older anonymous revelation were fused retrospectively.

The central documentary question is:

    Was Lakulīśa a real second-century teacher who was later declared to be Śiva—or did the Pāśupatas turn an anonymous possessed body into the founder they needed?

