Thumbnail text:

ŚIVA ENTERED HIM

Alternative:

THE FOUNDER IS MISSING

The opening title should be balanced within the first minute by explaining that corpse-entry is the sect’s sacred origin narrative, not externally verified biography.
Opening hook

    A dead Brahmin lies abandoned in a village in Gujarat.

    Śiva descends into the body.

    The corpse rises, takes up a wooden club and walks toward Ujjain.

    Four disciples gather around him, and one of India’s oldest Śaiva orders begins.

    Centuries later, stone inscriptions confirm that the disciples’ lineages really existed.

    But the earliest scripture never gives the resurrected teacher a name.

Visual sequence:

    Empty village road at dawn.

    A staff lying beside a covered human silhouette.

    Sanskrit: कायावतरण — kāyāvataraṇa, “descent into a body.”

    Lakulīśa sculptures from different regions.

    Mathura inscription dated 381 CE.

    Text: “HISTORY BEGINS AFTER THE MIRACLE.”

Why this subject is exceptionally valuable

This pack gives you six connected content engines.
1. A founder who enters a corpse

The origin resembles parakāyapraveśa, entry into another body, but the early commentary’s wording is more specifically divine descent into a deceased Brahmin’s body. It can be explored as theology, resurrection narrative and the production of charismatic authority—without presenting it as a reproducible yogic method.
2. A scripture that withholds the founder’s name

Kauṇḍinya’s Pañcārthabhāṣya says that the Lord assumed a human form at Kāyāvatara and instructed Kuśika, but the passage does not name Lakulīśa. Clearer identification of Lakulīśa as founder appears in subsequent Pāśupata and Purāṇic tradition. This is the story’s strongest scholarly reveal.
3. Deliberately antisocial sacred behaviour

Pāśupata practice included public conduct designed to attract mockery, abuse and social rejection. This was not random madness: the humiliation was interpreted as transferring merit and demerit between ascetic and aggressor. The ascetic weaponized society’s contempt.
4. Some of India’s earliest documented guru institutions

The Mathura pillar inscription of Gupta year 61—381 CE—records a Pāśupata lineage connected with Kuśika and the establishment of memorial shrines for teachers. It is unusually strong evidence for an organized lineage, although the inscription itself does not simply announce “Lakulīśa founded the Pāśupatas.”
5. A forgotten ancestor of later Śaiva tantra

The Pāśupatas predate Abhinavagupta’s mature nondual synthesis. Later Mantramārga and Bhairava traditions differed radically from them, but inherited a world already shaped by Śaiva initiation, ascetic authority, sectarian scripture and organized transmission.
6. A living modern revival

Kāyāvarohaṇa remains a pilgrimage site in Gujarat, with a modern temple and organizations that present Lakulīśa as Śiva’s twenty-eighth incarnation. These living traditions are valuable for contemporary reception, but their precise dates and continuous-lineage claims need independent historical evaluation.
The three Lakulīśas

The documentary should distinguish three overlapping figures.
The anonymous embodied Lord

In Kauṇḍinya’s commentary, Śiva assumes a human body at Kāyāvatara and teaches Kuśika. The foundational event exists, but the name Lakulīśa is absent from this crucial early passage.
The historical lineage founder

Later inscriptions and teacher genealogies suggest that a major teacher—or a remembered founder—stood several generations before the fourth-century Mathura Pāśupatas. Attempts to calculate his lifetime often place him around the second century CE, but that dating depends on treating lineage counts as reliable chronological units.
The divine Lakulīśa

Purāṇic traditions identify him as Śiva’s twenty-eighth and final incarnation of a recurring cycle, give him four disciples and connect him explicitly to Kāyāvarohaṇa. By this point, the teacher is no longer simply inspired by Śiva—he is Śiva.

These layers need not be treated as mutually exclusive from a believer’s standpoint, but historically they should not be collapsed.
Names and terminology
Lakulīśa

लकुलीश — lakulīśa

Usually explained as:

    lakula/laguḍa: club, staff or cudgel;

    īśa: lord.

Therefore:

    “Lord of the Club” or “The Staff-Bearing Lord.”

Variant spellings:

    Lakulīśa

    Lakulisha

    Lakulin

    Lakulī

    Nakulīśa in some transmission or scribal variants

    Lāguḍīśa in staff-based interpretations

Kāyāvarohaṇa

कायावरोहण — kāyāvarohaṇa

    kāya: body;

    avarohaṇa: descent.

Approximately:

    “Descent into the body.”

The site is also rendered:

    Kāyāvatāra;

    Kāyāvarohaṇa;

    Karvan;

    Karvāṇa;

    Kayavarohan.

The place-name itself reinforces the incarnation narrative.
Pāśupata

पाशुपत — pāśupata

Derived from Paśupati, “Lord of creatures” or “Lord of bound beings,” an epithet of Śiva.

The system distinguishes:

    paśu: the bound individual;

    pati: the sovereign Lord;

    the means by which bondage and suffering are ended.

Do not translate paśu merely as “animal.” In sectarian philosophy it refers to the dependent, bound self.
