Grade B — plausible historical reconstruction
Lakulīśa may have lived around the second century CE

Calculations based on the Mathura lineage place Kuśika near Lakulīśa and count multiple generations before the 381 inscription. If the succession is genealogically accurate and generations were of ordinary length, a second-century founder is plausible. But initiation lineages can omit, compress or symbolically organize teachers.
Kāyāvarohaṇa was an early Pāśupata centre

The textual narrative, site tradition and later regional evidence strongly connect the founder with Karvan/Kāyāvarohaṇa in Gujarat. The surviving modern temple is not itself an untouched ancient building, however, and the full archaeological sequence requires specialist confirmation.
A historical teacher may have been posthumously identified with Śiva

This pattern is plausible:

    historical charismatic teacher;

    lineage founder;

    divinely empowered teacher;

    bodily incarnation of Śiva;

    recurring cosmic avatāra.

The surviving evidence does not let us determine at which stage “Lakulīśa” became a divine name.
His teaching may have formalized an older Pāśupata movement

Pāśupata concepts and worship of Paśupati may predate Lakulīśa. Several scholars therefore describe him as organizer, reformer or first formal preceptor rather than absolute founder.
Grade C — sacred biography

These should be attributed to tradition:

    Śiva searched for and entered a dead Brahmin’s body.

    The reanimated body immediately became a perfect teacher.

    Lakulīśa was precisely Śiva’s twenty-eighth incarnation.

    Four named disciples dispersed to four regions exactly as later genealogies state.

    The teacher walked immediately from Kāyāvarohaṇa to Ujjain.

    His physical staff possessed supernatural power.

    Every Lakulīśa image reproduces his historical appearance.

    The modern lineage is institutionally unbroken from his lifetime.

Grade D — claims to avoid
“Lakulīśa founded Hinduism”

He belongs to one influential Śaiva current within a much larger and older religious landscape.
“The Pāśupatas were the first Śaivas”

Śiva/Rudra worship and other Śaiva developments predate the surviving Pāśupata institutions.
“The Mathura inscription proves Lakulīśa existed”

It proves an early teacher lineage connected with Kuśika. The precise historical identification of its ultimate founder remains interpretive, and scholars note that the inscription does not straightforwardly use every later sectarian label.
“His earliest scripture names him”

It does not, which is central to the mystery.
“Pāśupatas were just mad naked men”

Publicly disruptive behaviour was one stage within a structured discipline involving temple observance, meditation, ethical control, theological knowledge and eventual solitude.
“They deliberately harmed people”

The practice sought abuse directed toward the ascetic, not instructions to attack others.
“Corpse possession was a technique taught to all members”

The body-entry story concerns divine incarnation. This pack provides no practical material for attempting dangerous rites involving bodies, death sites or altered states.
The corpse-entry narrative

Kauṇḍinya’s commentary says, in substance, that the Lord took human form by inhabiting the body of a deceased Brahmin at Kāyāvatara and later reached Ujjain. The passage’s importance is not merely supernatural. It establishes that the teacher’s body has no ordinary social biography.
Why use a dead body?
It removes human parentage

The founder does not derive authority from:

    family;

    caste ancestry;

    biological birth;

    ordinary education;

    royal appointment.

His authority descends directly from Śiva.
It turns the body into an instrument

The body is not the ultimate self. It becomes a temporary vehicle occupied by divine agency.
It creates perfect discontinuity

A normal reformer emerges from a prior tradition. A god entering a dead body can introduce revelation without admitting a human source.
It sanctifies the place-name

Kāyāvarohaṇa becomes not only where the teacher lived but where divinity crossed into embodiment.
Responsible comparison

Later Indian traditions contain stories of consciousness entering another body, but one should not automatically project later technical systems onto this account. Here, the central action is Śiva’s descent, not an aspirant learning a body-transfer exercise.
The founder missing from his scripture

This should be the documentary’s midpoint reveal.

The Pāśupatasūtra is conventionally associated with Lakulīśa, yet:

    the text does not plainly identify him as author;

    Kauṇḍinya narrates the embodied Lord and Kuśika;

    Lakulīśa’s name appears clearly in later sectarian layers;

    later Purāṇas stabilize the four-disciple biography.

Three possible explanations
Model 1: Lakulīśa was always the intended Lord

The earliest community knew the identity orally, and the commentary did not need to spell it out.
Model 2: The anonymous Lord was later named Lakulīśa

A staff-bearing founder figure gradually absorbed the older incarnation narrative.
Model 3: Several teachers were combined

A historical Lakulīśa, an anonymous divine revealer and a lineage memory around Kuśika may have fused.

No current evidence completely resolves these models.
The four disciples

Later tradition gives Lakulīśa four principal disciples:

    Kuśika

    Gārgya or Garga

    Mitra

    Kauruṣya

Lists and spellings vary.
Kuśika

Kuśika has the strongest early institutional importance because the Mathura genealogy traces teachers through his succession. The commentary also names him as recipient of the Lord’s instruction.
Why four?

The four disciples may signify:

    actual early branches;

    geographical expansion;

    a symbolic four-direction mission;

    retrospective coordination of regional lineages;

    separate orders unified under one founder.

A useful visual is a map with four expanding routes—but label them traditional lineage geography, not reconstructed travel paths.
The yoga of deliberate humiliation

This is the strongest conceptual section.

At one stage, the Pāśupata practitioner was instructed to leave respectable temple behaviour and act publicly in ways that attracted contempt.

Descriptions include behaviour interpreted as:

    laughing without apparent cause;

    singing or dancing conspicuously;

    making unusual sounds;

    appearing asleep while awake;

    displaying socially awkward conduct;

    inviting accusations of madness or indecency.

Do not present these as a modern challenge or practical routine.
The sectarian logic

The ascetic sought to:

    break dependence on reputation;

    attract verbal abuse;

    remain inwardly controlled;

    transfer negative karmic consequences toward the aggressor;

    receive the aggressor’s merit;

    destroy the social identity that sustains attachment.

Strong narration line

    The Pāśupata did not merely endure humiliation. He turned humiliation into a ritual economy.

Ethical tension

From a modern perspective, deliberately manipulating bystanders into anger raises obvious ethical problems. The historical account should not be romanticized.

Questions to explore:

    Were people unknowingly used as ritual instruments?

    Did public performance challenge caste respectability?

    Was apparent madness protective camouflage?

    Did the practice allow ascetics to exist outside conventional labour and family structures?

    How did authorities distinguish a Pāśupata from a disruptive stranger?

Comparative frame

This can be compared cautiously with:

    Cynic philosophers violating convention;

    “holy fool” traditions;

    Tibetan smyon pa sacred madmen;

    avadhūtas;

    later antinomian Śaiva figures.

Do not claim direct historical influence without evidence.
Pāśupata philosophy in accessible form

The Pañcārthabhāṣya explains five major categories—hence “five meanings” or pañcārtha.
Kārya — effect

The dependent realm:

    individual souls;

    knowledge;

    bodily and material conditions;

    what is produced or governed.

Kāraṇa — cause

The independent Lord, Paśupati, who acts as sovereign cause.
Yoga — union or disciplined connection

The means by which the practitioner becomes joined to the Lord through awareness and practice.
Vidhi — prescribed conduct

The ordered disciplines, including temple observance and the later socially disruptive phase.
Duḥkhānta — end of suffering

Liberation understood as the ending of bondage and acquisition of extraordinary capacities under the Lord’s sovereignty.

The system is not simply nondualism. Pāśupata thought maintains a significant distinction between Lord and individual even in liberation, which later nondual Śaivas would rethink.
Link to Abhinavagupta

Avoid claiming a simple straight line:

Lakulīśa → Abhinavagupta.

The actual history involves multiple Śaiva streams.

A responsible genealogy is:

    early Atimārga traditions, especially Pāśupata;

    developments including Lākula and Kāpālika currents;

    expanding Mantramārga scripture and initiation;

    Siddhānta, Bhairava, Trika, Krama and Kaula systems;

    Abhinavagupta’s synthesis.

What later tantra inherited

Broadly:

    Śiva as supreme initiatory Lord;

    non-Vedic sectarian revelation;

    guru succession;

    specialized initiation;

    ascetic institutions;

    liberation conceptualized through Śiva’s power;

    a religious identity exceeding ordinary caste-household roles.

What changed

Later tantric Śaivism placed greater emphasis on:

    mantra;

    maṇḍala;

    deity installation;

    elaborate initiation;

    ritual cosmology;

    Śakti;

    multiple levels of scripture;

    in nondual systems, identity between awareness and Bhairava.

Strong bridge line

    Abhinavagupta’s Bhairava is philosophically far from a club-bearing ascetic provoking strangers in the street. Institutionally, however, he inhabits a Śaiva world the Pāśupatas helped create.

Mathura: the hard historical evidence

The Mathura pillar inscription is vital because it moves the video from legend into datable institution.
What it records

Scholarly interpretations identify:

    a date in Gupta year 61, corresponding to 381 CE;

    a Pāśupata teacher lineage;

    succession connected with Kuśika;

    establishment of a gurvāyatana, a shrine or sacred establishment for teachers;

    commemoration of deceased masters.

The exact reading and identification of a figure carved on the pillar have generated debate. Diwakar Acharya’s article specifically reassesses the supposed Lakulīśa image associated with the inscription.
