Recommended narrative structure
Act I — The corpse that stood up

Tell the Kāyāvarohaṇa story in its sacred form.

Do not begin by debunking it. Let the narrative establish its power.
Act II — The scripture refuses to name him

Open the Pañcārthabhāṣya.

Reveal that the embodied Lord appears, teaches Kuśika and travels onward—but “Lakulīśa” is absent.

This turns a miracle story into an identity investigation.
Act III — Searching for the historical founder

Present:

    the four disciples;

    Kuśika;

    Mathura;

    lineage generations;

    proposed second-century date;

    limits of genealogical calculation.

Act IV — The ascetics who wanted contempt

Explain the ordered stages of Pāśupata life.

Spend time on the ethical and psychological strangeness of humiliation practice.
Act V — What they actually believed

Explain:

    Paśupati;

    bound souls;

    God as independent cause;

    yoga;

    the ending of suffering;

    distinction between Lord and liberated soul.

Act VI — The founder becomes God

Show the iconographic transformation:

    staff-bearing teacher;

    divine yogi;

    avatāra of Śiva;

    temple deity.

Act VII — The movement spreads

Use sculptures and inscriptions across India.

Avoid implying every staff-bearing Śiva is definitely Lakulīśa.
Act VIII — The hidden ancestor of tantra

Move from Pāśupata institutional structures toward later Śaiva tantra and Abhinavagupta.

Clarify both continuity and rupture.
Act IX — Kāyāvarohaṇa today

Show how modern devotees understand the same story.

Present scholarly and devotional readings side by side without forcing either to impersonate the other.
Ending

    Perhaps a second-century teacher was gradually remembered as Śiva. Perhaps the sect always believed that no merely human teacher had founded it.

    Either way, Lakulīśa’s final trick was greater than entering a corpse. He disappeared inside the religion he helped create—leaving behind a staff, four lineages and a scripture that would not say his name.

