Research Pack 09: Līlāvajra — The Tantric Said to Have Built Kathmandu From a Single Tree
Core video thesis

Kathmandu tradition credits an almost unknown Buddhist tantric master named Līlāvajra with trapping a supernatural wish-fulfilling tree in human form, compelling it to surrender its timber, and using that single tree to construct Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa—the vast wooden pavilion from which Kathmandu supposedly received its name.

The legend contains a real archaeological shock: after Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa collapsed in the 2015 earthquake, scientific investigation dated parts of its foundation to approximately the early seventh through early ninth centuries—far earlier than the twelfth-century date commonly repeated before the excavation. That range overlaps surprisingly well with Newar traditions placing Līlāvajra in the eighth century. It does not prove that he built the structure or that one miraculous tree supplied all its timber, but it turns what looked like an obviously late foundation legend into a genuine historical mystery.

The investigation becomes stranger because “Līlāvajra” may refer to several overlapping identities:

    a Newar Vajrācārya associated with Śikhamu or Tarumūla Mahāvihāra;

    an eighth-century Indian Buddhist author also known as Vilāsavajra or Lalitavajra;

    a mahāsiddha linked with Uḍḍiyāna and Mañjuśrī practices;

    Lilāpā of the Eighty-Four Mahāsiddhas;

    and, in a competing Nāth legend, Lopipā, a yogi said to have acquired the magical tree from Kailāsa.

The strongest documentary question is:

    Did one historical Buddhist master build Kathmandu’s oldest monumental wooden structure—or did Buddhist, Nāth and civic traditions compete to claim the mysterious founder of the city itself?

