Thumbnail text:

ONE TREE BUILT THIS

Alternative:

THE CITY’S LOST FOUNDER

The thumbnail should use the real pavilion and an enormous tree silhouette rather than an AI-generated portrait presented as Līlāvajra.
Opening hook

    Kathmandu is named after a wooden building.

    That building stood for centuries in the centre of the old city until the 2015 earthquake reduced it to rubble.

    Tradition said it had been built from one impossible tree by a Buddhist tantric named Līlāvajra. Historians dismissed the story’s early date.

    Then archaeologists tested the foundations.

    The building was hundreds of years older than expected—and suddenly the forgotten tantric was standing in the right century.

Then qualify immediately:

    The scientific dating supports an early building. It does not prove the supernatural tree or identify its architect.

Why this subject is exceptional
1. Myth collides directly with archaeology

Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa was commonly dated to the twelfth century on the basis of later textual and architectural evidence. Post-earthquake excavations instead produced dates between the early seventh and early ninth centuries for foundational phases. That makes it one of the rare yogi legends where new archaeology materially changes how seriously the chronology must be considered.
2. The saint is attached to the identity of an entire city

“Kathmandu” derives through Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa, meaning approximately “wooden pavilion.” The Līlāvajra legend is therefore not merely a temple foundation tale; it explains the capital’s name and civic centre.
3. Buddhist and Nāth traditions compete over the founder

A community-based heritage study records both:

    the Buddhist account in which Siddha Līlāvajra obtains the tree through tantric power;

    a Nāth account in which Lopipā brings a magical tree from Kailāsa.

This suggests that the structure became a contested sacred object claimed by different religious histories.
4. The building later became associated with Gorakṣanātha

Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa eventually contained a shrine to Gorakṣanātha, commonly dated to a later phase. The building may therefore preserve successive layers:

    early public pavilion;

    Buddhist tantric foundation memory;

    Nāth appropriation or coexistence;

    Malla-period civic and festival use;

    modern national heritage.

Durham’s summary notes that the Gorakṣanātha shrine was conventionally dated centuries later than the newly identified foundational phase.
5. Līlāvajra may be more than one person

The Newar founder is sometimes identified with the eighth-century Indian Buddhist master Vilāsavajra/Lalitavajra, known in Tibetan traditions for works related to Mañjuśrī and the Guhyagarbha Tantra. That identification is possible but far from established.
The basic legend

Modern Newar retellings vary, but the broad structure is:

    A supernatural being or kalpavṛkṣa, wish-fulfilling tree, appears in human form.

    Līlāvajra recognizes its real identity.

    Through mantra or tantric knowledge, he prevents it from leaving.

    The being agrees to provide a tree in exchange for release.

    The tree is felled or supplied.

    Līlāvajra builds a gigantic wooden public pavilion.

    Enough timber remains to construct another structure.

    The pavilion becomes known as Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa or Maru Sataḥ.

    The name eventually becomes attached to Kathmandu itself.

A conservation study records a local version in which Līlāvajra “spellbound” the wish-fulfilling tree, received timber in return for releasing it, built Maru Sataḥ, and used the remaining wood for another pavilion.
Narrative warning

Do not merge every variation into a falsely precise original story. Versions disagree about:

    whether the tree itself assumes human form;

    whether a tree spirit or divine visitor is captured;

    where the tree grows;

    whether it comes from Kailāsa;

    how the bargain is made;

    which secondary building receives the remaining wood;

    whether Līlāvajra personally designs the structure;

    whether the builder is Buddhist Līlāvajra or Nāth Lopipā.

Evidence grading
Grade A — strong archaeological and architectural evidence
Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa had an early foundational phase

Post-disaster archaeological investigations involving Nepal’s Department of Archaeology and Durham/Stirling researchers produced dates indicating construction between roughly the early seventh and early ninth centuries. The structure therefore began substantially earlier than the twelfth-century date often previously assumed.
The building was a major wooden pavilion

Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa functioned as a large public shelter or sataḥ/sattal, later carrying social, religious and festival functions. It stood in Maru near Kathmandu Durbar Square and was closely tied to the city’s identity.
It collapsed in the 25 April 2015 earthquake

The structure was destroyed during the 2015 Nepal earthquake and subsequently studied and reconstructed using archaeological, community and heritage-conservation methods.
Its history contains multiple phases

Scientific and architectural work indicates that Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa was maintained, modified and repaired over many centuries rather than surviving as one unchanged construction.
A Līlāvajra foundation tradition exists

Nepalese Vajrācārya publications and community narratives explicitly identify Līlāvajra as the builder or founding siddha connected with Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa. The tradition is real as cultural memory even though its antiquity and historical accuracy require further proof.
Grade B — plausible historical reconstruction
A historical Līlāvajra may have lived in eighth-century Kathmandu

Newar tradition places him around the reign of the Licchavi ruler Jayadeva II and associates him with Śikhamu/Tarumūla Mahāvihāra near Maru. Scientific dates for the pavilion now make an eighth-century builder chronologically possible.

This is correspondence, not identification.

The archaeological dates do not name an architect.
A Buddhist institution may have been involved in the building’s origin

The founder tradition, nearby vihāra affiliations and later Newar memory make Buddhist participation plausible. However, the original structure may have been a civic shelter supported by several communities rather than one sectarian temple.
One exceptionally large timber source may lie behind the “single tree” story

A huge tree could theoretically have supplied several principal columns or a memorable portion of the structure. Detailed dendrological analysis would be needed to determine:

    whether surviving timbers came from one tree;

    whether they belong to multiple construction phases;

    whether later repairs replaced original elements.

The legend’s “one tree” may preserve:

    literal memory of extraordinary timber;

    ritual symbolism of a cosmic tree;

    exaggeration of a major donation;

    unification of many construction phases into one founding event.

Līlāvajra may have been associated with Mañjuśrī practice

Newar biographies connect him with the Mañjuśrīnāmasaṃgīti, while the Indian/Tibetan Līlāvajra or Vilāsavajra is known as a commentator in Mañjuśrī and tantric traditions. This overlap may support identification—or may have caused two similar figures to be fused.
Grade C — lineage tradition and hagiography

Present these as stories, not verified events:

    Līlāvajra instantly recognized a supernatural tree-being.

    He immobilized it using mantra.

    The being surrendered one miraculous tree.

    Every part of the enormous building came from that single trunk.

    Leftover wood built another full structure.

    He travelled supernaturally between Nepal, India and Uḍḍiyāna.

    He was a direct disciple of Lakṣmīṅkarā.

    He was unquestionably one of the canonical Eighty-Four Mahāsiddhas.

    He was simultaneously the author known to Tibetan tradition as Vilāsavajra.

    His construction directly caused the entire city to be called Kathmandu immediately.

Grade D — misleading claims to avoid
“Archaeology proved Līlāvajra built Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa”

Archaeology established an early construction date. It did not identify the builder.
“Carbon dating proved the single-tree miracle”

Scientific dating and timber-provenance analysis are separate questions.
“Kathmandu was founded in the eighth century”

The valley and its settlements are substantially older. The pavilion helped name the later city; it did not create urban civilization from nothing.
“Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa was originally a Buddhist temple”

Its earliest function may have been a public pavilion. Later religious installations do not necessarily define its original use.
“Līlāvajra and Vilāsavajra were definitely the same man”

The shared or similar names and approximate period are suggestive but insufficient.
“Lilāpā of the Eighty-Four Siddhas built Kathmandu”

This is a lineage identification that needs source criticism.
“Gorakṣanātha founded the building”

The Nāth shrine appears to belong to a later layer, though Nāth traditions developed their own foundation account.
“The whole structure used no nails”

Popular architectural claims should be verified against the reconstruction and technical survey; timber buildings can use sophisticated joinery while still containing metal introduced during later repairs.
Who was Līlāvajra?
Name

लीलावज्र — līlāvajra

    līlā: play, divine play, spontaneous manifestation;

    vajra: diamond, thunderbolt or indestructible awakened power.

Possible translation:

    “Vajra of Divine Play” or “Indestructible Play.”

Newar spellings and romanizations include:

    Lila Bajra;

    Lila Vajra;

    Leela Vajra;

    Līlāvajra;

    Līlapa;

    Lilapa.

Reported Newar biography

Modern Newar accounts place him:

    in the eighth century;

    during the Licchavi period;

    at Śikhamu or Tarumūla Mahāvihāra;

    near the Maru district;

    as a tantric Buddhist master;

    as builder of Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa;

    as a contributor to caryā song and ritual culture.

A recent community biography gives a precise date around 743 CE, but such precision should not be accepted without an inscription, manuscript colophon or early chronicle.
The major problem

The name is not unique.

At least three possible identities circulate:
1. Newar Līlāvajra

Kathmandu siddha and alleged builder.
2. Indian Vilāsavajra/Līlāvajra/Lalitavajra

An eighth-century tantric Buddhist author, possibly from Uḍḍiyāna, associated with:

    the Mañjuśrīnāmasaṃgīti;

    the Guhyagarbha Tantra;

    Buddhaguhya;

    Buddhajñānapāda.

3. Lilāpā, mahāsiddha

A figure in later Eighty-Four Mahāsiddha cycles. The relationship between this hagiographic siddha, the Indian textual author and Kathmandu’s builder is not settled.
The Indian–Newar identity problem
Evidence favouring identification

    Similar names: Līlāvajra, Vilāsavajra, Lalitavajra.

    Roughly compatible eighth-century dating.

    Mañjuśrī-related practices.

    Trans-Himalayan Buddhist scholarly movement.

    Newar traditions frequently incorporated Indian tantric masters.

    Vilāsavajra’s pupils were major figures in Buddhist tantric transmission.

Evidence against confident identification

    No cited inscription links the Indian author with Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa.

    “Līlāvajra” may be an initiatory name reusable by multiple masters.

    Newar biographies may have absorbed prestigious Indian lineages retrospectively.

    The alleged teachers attributed to the Kathmandu master differ across accounts.

    Tibetan textual catalogues may preserve no mention of Kathmandu architecture.

    A local builder and an international scholar could simply share a name.

Best documentary formulation

    In Tibetan libraries, Līlāvajra is an Indian tantric author. In Kathmandu, he is the siddha who built the city’s wooden heart. The surviving evidence has not yet proved that these are the same man.

The Lakṣmīṅkarā connection

Modern Newar accounts call Līlāvajra a disciple of the female mahāsiddha Lakṣmīṅkarā, often described as a princess of Uḍḍiyāna.

This is attractive narratively because it creates a lineage:

Uḍḍiyāna princess → travelling tantric master → monumental Kathmandu pavilion

But every link requires investigation.
Research questions

    What is the earliest source naming Lakṣmīṅkarā as his guru?

    Does the claim appear in a Newar manuscript or only recent biographies?

    Is this Līlāvajra the same one listed after Lakṣmīṅkarā in tantric author chronologies?

    Does Tibetan hagiography record their teacher–disciple relationship?

    Was “Lakṣmīṅkarā” used to confer Uḍḍiyāna prestige on a local lineage?

Do not call the relationship historical until an early textual witness is located.
Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa: building, pavilion and city-name
Name

काष्ठमण्डप — kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa

    kāṣṭha: wood or timber;

    maṇḍapa: hall, pavilion or covered assembly structure.

Approximately:

    “Wooden Pavilion.”

The Nepal Bhasa name Maru Sataḥ is often interpreted as the pavilion or shelter at Maru. “Maru” has attracted folk explanations involving emptiness or absence of a central image, but the historical etymology should be checked with Nepal Bhasa specialists.
Original function

The building appears to have functioned as:

    a public shelter;

    assembly space;

    rest house;

    festival site;

    religious structure in later phases;

    urban landmark.

Calling it only a “temple” oversimplifies its civic role.
The city name

The name Kathmandu developed from Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa through historical pronunciation and usage. The building became so central that outsiders increasingly used its name for the surrounding city.

Strong line:

    Līlāvajra’s legend does not merely give him a building. It gives him the word by which the world knows Nepal’s capital.

What archaeology changed after 2015
Before excavation

Many summaries dated Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa to around the twelfth century, partly because the building’s documented later history and standing architecture belonged heavily to medieval phases.
After the collapse

The destruction exposed foundational deposits and allowed:

    archaeological excavation;

    radiocarbon analysis;

    optically stimulated luminescence dating;

    documentation of construction phases;

    assessment of earlier earthquake survival;

    detailed study of the building’s structural failures.

Result

The foundations indicated construction sometime between approximately the early seventh and early ninth centuries.
What this proves

    An important structure existed there much earlier than often thought.

    The site survived multiple seismic events and repair cycles.

    Kathmandu possessed monumental timber architecture during the Licchavi or transitional period.

    An eighth-century Līlāvajra is chronologically possible.

What it does not prove

    The exact construction year.

    A single architect.

    Buddhist ownership.

    the single-tree claim.

    supernatural intervention.

    identification with Vilāsavajra.

    continuity of every later tradition back to the first phase.

The “single tree” test

This is an excellent evidence-led segment.
Questions for timber specialists

    How many original structural elements survived before 2015?

    Were samples taken from multiple posts and beams?

    Can dendrochronology establish whether major timbers came from one tree?

    Were the principal columns of matching species and age?

    How many repair phases replaced original timber?

    Could one extremely large trunk plausibly supply the earliest core?

    Does “one tree” mean one trunk, one grove, one royal timber grant or one sacred source?

    Were reused timbers identified?

    Did the reconstruction preserve and catalogue every surviving element?

    Is a public technical timber report available?

Possible outcome

Even disproving the literal single-tree claim would not destroy the legend. It could reveal that “one tree” symbolized:

    the cosmic tree;

    unity of the city;

    one miraculous patron;

    one ancestral forest source;

    or one founding construction phase later expanded.

The competing Nāth version: Lopipā

A heritage reconstruction study notes a narrative found in a manuscript or pilgrimage tradition called Śrīnāth Pilgrimages, in which Lopipā, identified as a Nāth siddha, obtains the magical tree from Kailāsa and constructs Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa.
Why this matters

This is not merely a variant spelling.

It potentially represents competition between:

    Newar Buddhist ownership of the building;

    Nāth yogic ownership through Gorakṣanātha;

    civic tradition treating the pavilion as shared space;

    later Hindu royal narratives.

Possible relationships
Model 1: Līlāvajra became Lopipā

A Buddhist siddha’s name and biography were absorbed into Nāth tradition.
Model 2: Lopipā became Līlāvajra

A Nāth founder legend was Buddhistized by Newar Vajrācāryas.
Model 3: Separate stories fused

The old structure attracted multiple sacred founders.
Model 4: Both descend from a lost shared siddha story

A wandering tantric builder preceded firm Buddhist/Nāth categorization.
Strong video line

    Kathmandu did not merely forget who built its oldest pavilion. Two yogic traditions remembered different founders—and each placed its own siddha at the birth of the city.

Gorakṣanātha inside a Buddhist founder story

Later Kāṣṭhamaṇḍapa housed a shrine to Gorakṣanātha. Durham’s historical summary notes that this installation was conventionally associated with a later medieval date than the early foundations.
Key interpretations

    Nāths may have taken over or gained ritual presence in an older public building.

    The pavilion may always have been shared across traditions.

    The Gorakṣanātha installation may have generated the competing Lopipā foundation story.

    Royal patronage could have reclassified an earlier Buddhist civic monument.

    Newar religious space frequently resists clean modern labels.

Avoid describing this automatically as hostile “Hindu appropriation.” Shared use, gradual institutional change and political patronage are all possible.
