Research Pack 10: Pūrṇānanda — The Forgotten Tantric Behind the Modern Chakra System
Core video thesis

The rainbow “seven chakra” system known across global yoga culture did not descend unchanged from prehistoric India. One of its most important textual ancestors was a short Sanskrit work composed in Bengal’s Śākta-tantric world by an almost biographically invisible author named Pūrṇānanda.

His Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa—“Explanation of the Six Centres”—describes six bodily lotuses, an ascending central channel, Kuṇḍalinī and a transcendent thousand-petalled region above them. Centuries later, the British colonial judge John Woodroffe, writing as Arthur Avalon, translated it in The Serpent Power. That publication helped turn one relatively late tantric map into the best-known chakra model on Earth. The Sanskrit text survives independently, while Muktabodha identifies Pūrṇānanda’s larger Śrītattvārthacintāmaṇi as a major Bengali Śākta compendium.

The documentary’s central question:

    How did one obscure Bengali tantric’s six-centre ritual map become the supposedly universal seven-chakra anatomy of modern spirituality?

This is not a debunking of chakras. It is a history of how one tradition-specific system became globally standardized.
