That framing is stronger and safer than claiming Pūrṇānanda single-handedly “invented” chakras, because chakra systems existed before him and differed significantly between texts.
Opening hook

    Ask almost anyone to draw the human energy body and they will give you the same image: seven coloured circles running from the base of the spine to the crown of the head.

    But one of the most influential Sanskrit sources behind that diagram is called The Explanation of the Six Chakras.

    It was written by a tantric scholar named Pūrṇānanda, about whom almost nothing is known.

    Then a British High Court judge translated it—and one regional Śākta system began to look like the universal anatomy of the human soul.

Visual sequence:

    Modern rainbow chakra poster.

    The number 7.

    Sanskrit title: षट्चक्रनिरूपण — ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa.

    ṣaṭ = six highlighted.

    Arthur Avalon’s The Serpent Power.

    Old lotus diagrams replacing modern coloured circles.

    Text: “WHEN DID SIX BECOME SEVEN?”

Why this is a major channel subject

This story connects several high-value areas:

    Bengali Śākta tantra;

    Kuṇḍalinī and subtle-body history;

    colonial Orientalism;

    manuscript transmission;

    modern yoga’s creation;

    global occultism;

    the difference between scripture and popular psychology;

    how diagrams become more powerful than texts;

    the erasure of an Indian author behind a famous Western publication.

It also provides a powerful corrective:

    There was never one fixed “ancient chakra system.” There were multiple ritual anatomies created for different deities, mantras, lineages and contemplative aims.

Pūrṇānanda’s scheme became globally dominant not necessarily because every Indian tradition accepted it, but because it was translated, diagrammed, printed and repeatedly copied.
Who was Pūrṇānanda?
What is reasonably secure

Pūrṇānanda was a Sanskrit tantric author connected with the late medieval or early modern Bengali Śākta environment. His Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa is generally dated to 1577 on the basis of the textual tradition used by later editors. The work survives in Sanskrit and was transmitted as part of, or in connection with, his larger Śrītattvārthacintāmaṇi.

He is credited with or associated with:

    Śrītattvārthacintāmaṇi;

    Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa;

    other Śākta ritual material requiring manuscript verification;

    a lineage rooted in eastern Indian tantra.

What remains uncertain

Publicly accessible sources do not currently establish with confidence:

    his birth date;

    his birthplace;

    his family;

    his social position;

    where he studied;

    his initiation lineage;

    whether he lived in Bengal proper, Kāmarūpa or moved between them;

    which goddess he regarded as his principal deity;

    where he died;

    whether any portrait or personal object survives.

This absence is part of the documentary:

    One of the most globally influential authors in yoga history survives almost entirely as a name attached to a technical text.

Name

पूर्णानन्द — pūrṇānanda

    pūrṇa: complete, full;

    ānanda: bliss.

Approximately:

    “Complete Bliss.”

This may have been an initiatory or renunciant name rather than a birth name.
Evidence grading
Grade A — strong textual evidence
The Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa exists as an independent Sanskrit text

A Sanskrit edition is available through Internet Archive and is marked there as public domain.

