Pūrṇānanda’s larger Śākta compendium was preserved in Bengali tantric textual collections

Muktabodha explicitly lists the Bengali Śrītattvārthacintāmaṇi of Pūrṇānanda alongside Kṛṣṇānanda’s Bṛhattantrasāra as a major late Śākta compendium containing information on goddess worship and related ritual systems.

Raw URL:

https://muktalib7.com/DL_CATALOG_ROOT/digital_library.htm
The text describes six principal bodily centres

The title itself identifies six cakras. The thousand-petalled region is positioned beyond or above those six, which helps explain how later summaries converted the structure into a list of seven major centres.
Pūrṇānanda’s scheme is not identical to popular rainbow charts

Modern rainbow colour assignments, psychological keywords, crystals, endocrine-gland correspondences and “blocked chakra” wellness language are not simply reproduced from the Sanskrit work. Even modern practitioner research notes the substantial difference between the text’s mantra-deity symbolism and contemporary chakra presentations.
Grade B — strong historical inference
Woodroffe’s translation was a major route by which this system entered English-language occult and yoga culture

The Serpent Power made an annotated English translation and diagrams widely available. The fact that many later English chakra books use the same sequence, petals, seed syllables and lotuses strongly suggests substantial dependence on this publication, although each later author should be traced separately.
Pūrṇānanda probably drew on earlier tantric traditions

He did not create subtle-body concepts from nothing. Earlier Śaiva, Śākta, Buddhist and yogic texts discuss:

    channels;

    winds;

    lotuses;

    bodily centres;

    Kuṇḍalinī-like powers;

    ascending ritual processes.

His importance lies in organizing one especially memorable synthesis.
His religious environment was Bengali Śākta rather than generic “Hindu yoga”

Muktabodha groups his work among later northern—especially Bengali—Śrīvidyā, Śākta and Kaula textual compendia. This gives a more historically specific setting than the modern label “ancient yogic science.”
Kāmarūpa and Assam may be important to his intellectual geography

Kāmarūpa was a major centre of goddess tantra, and manuscripts or regional traditions may connect Pūrṇānanda with the Bengal–Assam tantric corridor. This connection requires manuscript and lineage research rather than being asserted solely from modern biographies.
Grade C — later traditional reconstruction

Treat cautiously:

    Pūrṇānanda received the entire chakra system through supernatural revelation.

    He belonged to one specific modern lineage claiming him.

    His six centres describe universal physical organs.

    He personally intended Sahasrāra to be called “the seventh chakra.”

    Every diagram published under his name reproduces his original manuscript.

    His date can be narrowed to an exact day without consulting the relevant colophon.

    the system was unchanged from Vedic antiquity.

Grade D — misleading claims to reject
“Pūrṇānanda invented chakras”

Chakra and subtle-body systems predate him.

Better:

    He composed one of the most influential surviving formulations of the six-centre Kuṇḍalinī system.

“All Indian traditions teach seven chakras”

They do not. Systems vary by text and lineage.
“The chakras correspond scientifically to endocrine glands”

That is a modern comparative claim, not a conclusion established by Pūrṇānanda’s Sanskrit text.
“Each chakra has always had a rainbow colour”

The now-standard red-to-violet palette is not the organizing scheme of the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa.
“Ṣaṭ-cakra means seven chakras”

It literally means six centres. Sahasrāra occupies a distinct transcendent position above the six.
“Arthur Avalon merely copied the Sanskrit neutrally”

Woodroffe translated, selected, interpreted and presented the work through early twentieth-century Orientalist and occult categories. His edition is essential, but it is not transparent access to an untouched original.
“The text is a beginner’s meditation manual”

It presupposes a ritual and initiatory environment. This pack does not provide instructions for attempting intense breath, retention or forced energy practices.
How six became seven
Pūrṇānanda’s architecture

The sequence commonly extracted from the text is:

    Mūlādhāra

    Svādhiṣṭhāna

    Maṇipūra

    Anāhata

    Viśuddha

    Ājñā

Above them is:

    Sahasrāra, the thousand-petalled lotus;

    a supreme or transcendent region;

    the culmination rather than necessarily another equivalent centre.

The editorial transformation

When represented as a vertical diagram, the visual logic becomes irresistible:

    six circles below;

    one lotus above;

    therefore seven points.

The conceptual distinction between six centres traversed and a transcendent culmination disappears into a numbered chart.
Strong narration line

    The seventh chakra may have been created less by theology than by graphic design.

That line should be marked as an interpretive thesis, not a direct quotation from scholarship.
Why the seven-centre diagram won

Possible reasons:

    seven is culturally memorable;

    it fits existing Western sevenfold correspondences;

    it produces a clean vertical chart;

    occultists could pair it with colours, planets and psychological stages;

    printable illustrations travelled more easily than Sanskrit commentary;

    teacher-training systems needed a standardized curriculum.

This is a broader research programme: trace each correspondence to the first printed source that used it.
What the original system actually emphasizes

The text’s world is built from:

    Sanskrit letters;

    seed syllables;

    deities;

    goddess powers;

    geometric forms;

    elements;

    subtle channels;

    lotus petals;

    mantra;

    cosmological correspondences;

    the union or ascent of Kuṇḍalinī.

It is not primarily a system of:

    self-esteem;

    communication skills;

    financial security;

    relationship compatibility;

    personality typing;

    coloured crystals;

    scented oils.

Those modern meanings can be meaningful within modern practice, but they should not be projected backward as Pūrṇānanda’s own system.
Key documentary contrast

Place two charts side by side.
Modern chart

    rainbow colours;

    short emotional keywords;

    clean anatomical icons;

    “balance” language.

Pūrṇānanda chart

    Sanskrit phonemes;

    lotuses with specified petals;

    deities and śaktis;

    mantra syllables;

    ritual cosmology;

    Sahasrāra as a qualitatively different culmination.

The contrast alone could support a highly shareable short video.
Pūrṇānanda’s larger work

The Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa should not be treated as an isolated pamphlet floating outside a tradition.

Muktabodha identifies Pūrṇānanda’s Śrītattvārthacintāmaṇi as a major Bengali tantric compendium concerned with goddess worship and late Śākta ritual culture.
