    what initiatory assumptions the text made;

    how the subtle body related to external worship;

    whether later publishers detached the chakra section from its original ritual context;

    Pūrṇānanda’s relationship with other Bengali tantric compilations.

Central thesis

    Modern culture inherited the body-map but lost the book that explained what the map was for.

Arthur Avalon and the colonial transmission
Who was Arthur Avalon?

“Arthur Avalon” was the pen name associated with Sir John Woodroffe, a British judge and scholar working in colonial India.

His tantric publications tried to present Indian Śākta traditions as serious philosophical and religious systems rather than superstition. At the same time, his editorial choices helped create a canon of “Tantra” for global English readers.
Why The Serpent Power mattered

It offered:

    Sanskrit text;

    English translation;

    diagrams;

    commentary;

    explanatory introductions;

    a portable printed chakra system.

Internet Archive records the work as containing the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa and Pādukāpañcaka, explicitly presenting them as works on Laya Yoga.
The historical paradox

Pūrṇānanda supplied the Sanskrit formulation.

Woodroffe supplied:

    international publication;

    English terminology;

    explanatory architecture;

    visibility in Western occult and yoga networks.

The Indian author became obscure while the colonial translator became famous.
Responsible framing

Do not reduce Woodroffe to “a British thief.” His work helped preserve and publicize texts and was developed through collaboration with Indian scholars. But colonial privilege affected whose name appeared on the internationally circulated book and whose intellectual labour remained less visible.
Research questions

    Which Indian paṇḍits worked on the edition?

    Who prepared the Sanskrit text?

    Who drew the diagrams?

    Which Bengali editions did Woodroffe use?

    Did his collaborators leave memoirs or marginal notes?

    How much of the commentary came from named Indian informants?

    Why were their names less prominent than “Arthur Avalon”?

