Research Pack 11: Kṛṣṇānanda Āgamavāgīśa — The Tantric Who Gave Bengal Its Face of Kālī
Core video thesis

Bengal’s now-iconic image of Kālī—dark, unbound hair, tongue extended, one foot placed upon Śiva—is popularly traced to a nearly forgotten tantric compiler named Kṛṣṇānanda Āgamavāgīśa.

According to the famous Nabadwip legend, Kṛṣṇānanda prayed for a form of the formless Goddess that ordinary householders could worship. He then saw a dark village woman standing with one foot forward while placing fuel cakes on a wall. Embarrassed to be observed, she extended or bit her tongue. The tantric interpreted her posture as the Goddess’s answer and fashioned the familiar image of Dakṣiṇā Kālī.

That story is powerful—but historically unstable.

Kālī iconography existed long before Kṛṣṇānanda. Images and texts already portrayed her as dark, terrifying, dishevelled and associated with Śiva, battlefields and marginal spaces. What Kṛṣṇānanda appears to have done was more subtle and arguably more influential:

    He helped systematize a ritual form through which a frightening tantric goddess could enter respectable Bengali domestic worship.

His Tantrasāra became a major compilation of ritual material gathered from numerous Śaiva, Śākta, Vaiṣṇava, Gāṇapatya and solar sources. Yet his dates vary by almost a century across modern accounts, the village-woman story is difficult to trace to an early source, and several supposedly direct disciples lived too late to have known him personally.

The central documentary question is:

    Did Kṛṣṇānanda create Bengal’s Kālī—or did Bengal later make him the human author of a transformation that actually unfolded over centuries?

