Opening hook

    Bengal’s most recognizable goddess may have begun, according to legend, with an awkward moment beside a village wall.

    A tantric scholar had begged the formless Goddess to reveal a form that ordinary people could worship.

    At dawn, he saw a dark-skinned woman at work. One foot was forward. Her hair was loose. When she noticed him looking, she stuck out her tongue in embarrassment.

    The scholar fell to the ground.

    He believed the Goddess had answered.

    His name was Kṛṣṇānanda Āgamavāgīśa—and the image he supposedly created became Bengal’s Kālī.

Then qualify:

    The legend is famous. The historical evidence tells a more complicated story.

Why this subject is unusually valuable
1. It explains how visual religion is created

The story is not merely about theology. It concerns:

    who determines a deity’s appearance;

    how ritual descriptions become sculpture;

    how village bodies enter elite religious art;

    how one image defeats competing forms;

    how repetition eventually makes an invented convention appear timeless.

2. It joins tantra with domestic religion

Kālī’s older associations include:

    battle;

    death;

    the margins of society;

    yoginīs;

    fierce ritual;

    cremation-ground imagery.

The Bengali Dakṣiṇā Kālī remained visually frightening while being increasingly approached as a loving mother and household protector.

This is not simply “terror becoming kindness.” It is the domestication of terror without removing its symbols.
3. It is a story about compilation rather than revelation alone

Kṛṣṇānanda’s main historical significance may lie in his attempt to organize a vast and inconsistent tantric archive into a usable ritual compendium.

Modern accounts say his Bṛhat Tantrasāra drew material from approximately 170 tantric works. The precise number needs checking against the text’s introduction and manuscript tradition, but its breadth is widely emphasized in Bengali reception.
4. It exposes impossible guru genealogies

Kṛṣṇānanda is sometimes called the guru of Ramprasad Sen, the famous eighteenth-century Kālī poet. But many proposed dates place Kṛṣṇānanda in the sixteenth or early seventeenth century, while Ramprasad was born around the 1720s.

This may indicate:

    indirect textual influence;

    a later descendant bearing the same name;

    lineage affiliation expressed as direct discipleship;

    simple hagiographic compression.

The contradiction is a strong investigative hook.
5. His cult remains geographically alive

Agameśvarī Kālī is still worshipped at Nabadwip, and contemporary Bengali video and festival culture explicitly connects the deity with Kṛṣṇānanda. Physical sites identified as his ritual seat are also publicly commemorated, although their continuous historical provenance requires verification.
Names and meanings
Kṛṣṇānanda

कृष्णानन्द — kṛṣṇānanda

Possible meaning:

    kṛṣṇa: dark, black, or the deity Kṛṣṇa;

    ānanda: bliss.

Approximately:

    “Bliss of the Dark One” or “Bliss of Kṛṣṇa.”

The ambiguity is striking for a scholar associated with the dark Goddess while living in a region dominated by Caitanya Vaiṣṇavism.
Āgamavāgīśa

आगमवागीश — āgamavāgīśa

    āgama: transmitted scripture, especially tantric revelation;

    vāgīśa: lord or master of speech.

Approximately:

    “Master of the Speech of the Āgamas.”

This is probably an honorific title recognizing scriptural mastery rather than a family name.

Variants include:

    Agamavagisha;

    Agambagish;

    Agamvagish;

    Āgamavāgīśa;

    Krishnananda Bhattacharya.

Agameśvarī

The Kālī associated with him is called:

आगमेश्वरी — āgameśvarī

“Lady of the Āgamas” or “Mistress of transmitted tantric revelation.”

The name simultaneously links the deity to tantra and to Āgamavāgīśa himself.
The chronology problem

Modern sources offer several incompatible dates.
Early-sixteenth-century version

Some popular accounts make him a contemporary or fellow student of Caitanya, whose life is conventionally dated 1486–1533.

This would place Kṛṣṇānanda around the late fifteenth or early sixteenth century.
Mid-sixteenth-century version

Some sources give a birth date around 1533 and place his mature activity around the later sixteenth century.
Early-seventeenth-century version

Bengali summaries often place his birth around 1600–1610 and composition of the Tantrasāra around 1630–1640.
Even later devotional dating

Some online community retellings place him around 1650, which would push his career toward the later seventeenth century.
What can safely be concluded

Kṛṣṇānanda most likely belonged to the late medieval or early modern Bengali tantric world, probably between the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries.

Exact dating should remain open until the following are examined:

    earliest dated manuscripts;

    colophons;

    genealogical records;

    quotations by securely dated authors;

    family records from Nabadwip;

    references in contemporary Vaiṣṇava literature;

    early printed editions and editorial introductions.

Strong documentary line

    Kṛṣṇānanda is remembered as the man who fixed Kālī’s form, yet history cannot even fix the century in which he lived.

Evidence grading
Grade A — strong textual and cultural evidence
A tantric author named Kṛṣṇānanda Āgamavāgīśa existed

A substantial ritual compendium is transmitted under his name, and Bengali literary and ritual traditions consistently recognize him as a major tantric compiler.
The Tantrasāra became influential

The work survives in Sanskrit and Bengali editorial or translated traditions and serves as a wide-ranging compendium of deity worship, mantra, ritual procedure and tantric iconography.

Modern bibliographic descriptions emphasize its incorporation of material from different sectarian traditions rather than one exclusive cult.
Kṛṣṇānanda became closely associated with Dakṣiṇā Kālī

Both Bengali devotional memory and modern scholarship on Kālī’s reception regularly associate his name with the popularization or codification of Dakṣiṇā Kālī worship in Bengal.
Agameśvarī Kālī remains a living cult at Nabadwip

Contemporary festival footage, regional reporting and local commemorative sites identify the deity with Kṛṣṇānanda.
Physical sites are attributed to him

Wikimedia Commons hosts photographs identified by their uploader as the pañcamuṇḍi āsana associated with his Agameśvarī worship. This proves a present-day attribution and physical commemorative site, not uninterrupted provenance from his lifetime.
Grade B — plausible historical reconstruction
He helped standardize domestic Kālī worship

The Tantrasāra’s ritual compilation and his lasting association with Agameśvarī make it plausible that his tradition helped regularize forms of Kālī worship accessible to householders.

“Domestic” does not mean stripped of tantra. Household worship could still involve:

    mantras;

    initiation;

    ritual diagrams;

    complex offerings;

    priestly expertise;

    sectarian restrictions.

His work contributed to Bengal’s emerging Śākta public culture

Kālī worship became increasingly visible through household rites, aristocratic patronage, images and later public festivals. Kṛṣṇānanda’s codification may have been one ingredient in this transformation.

He should not be credited with causing the entire change alone.
His name became attached to a visual convention

It is plausible that his school promoted a recognizable Dakṣiṇā Kālī icon based on textual descriptions.

It is less plausible that no one had ever represented Kālī in comparable form before him.
He probably lived amid intense Śākta–Vaiṣṇava competition and exchange

Nabadwip was a major centre of Sanskrit scholarship and Caitanya Vaiṣṇavism. Kṛṣṇānanda’s later biographies often dramatize conflict between Vaiṣṇava and Śākta identities, including stories about his own family.

The historical extent of that conflict must be checked against contemporary sources rather than assumed from later sectarian storytelling.
Grade C — hagiography and later legend

These elements should be introduced as tradition:

    the Goddess instructed him in a dream to seek her physical form;

    the first woman he saw at dawn precisely embodied Kālī;

    he immediately created the definitive Kālī image;

    no anthropomorphic Kālī image had previously existed;

    the village woman was herself the Goddess;

    all Bengali Kālī worship descends directly from his ritual;

    he transformed dangerous tantra into completely harmless devotion;

    Ramprasad Sen personally studied under him;

    he entered final meditative absorption at a specific location;

    his ritual seat contains continuous supernatural power.

Grade D — claims to avoid
“Kṛṣṇānanda invented the goddess Kālī”

Kālī appears in much older Sanskrit religious literature and iconography.
“He created the first ever image of Kālī”

This is extremely difficult to sustain archaeologically.

Better:

    Later Bengali tradition credited him with fixing or popularizing the form of Dakṣiṇā Kālī that became dominant in Bengal.

“The village woman was a low-caste woman”

Some retellings emphasize social marginality, skin colour or occupation; others do not provide reliable caste identification.

Do not assign a caste without a source.
“The woman stuck out her tongue because that was a universal sign of shame”

Gesture meanings vary regionally and historically. Some versions say she bit her tongue; others say she extended it.
“Kālī’s tongue always represents embarrassment”

Older and alternative interpretations include:

    blood;

    hunger;

    consuming violence;

    shame after stepping on Śiva;

    the power of speech;

    rajas controlled by white teeth;

    later devotional symbolism.

“He removed all transgressive practices from tantra”

His compendium contains sophisticated and sometimes socially restricted ritual material. Domestic standardization is not equivalent to complete sanitization.
“His Tantrasāra is a Kashmir Śaiva text”

It must not be confused with Abhinavagupta’s prose Tantrasāra. Kṛṣṇānanda’s work belongs to a later Bengali tantric ritual environment.
“He was Ramprasad’s direct historical guru”

Present this as a traditional claim with a severe chronological problem.
The village-woman revelation
The common narrative

Kṛṣṇānanda longs for a visible form of Kālī appropriate for worship.

The Goddess tells him:

    The first female form you see at dawn will reveal me.

He encounters a woman carrying out ordinary domestic labour. She is generally described as:

    dark-skinned;

    with loose hair;

    one foot raised or placed forward;

    one hand lifted;

    working with cow-dung fuel;

    tongue extended in embarrassment after noticing him.

Kṛṣṇānanda recognizes:

    her dark body as Kālī;

    her dishevelled hair as the goddess’s freedom;

    her stance as the ālīḍha posture;

    her tongue as Kālī’s famous protruding tongue;

    her ordinary labour as divine revelation inside everyday life.

What makes the story profound

The form of the supreme Goddess does not descend through:

    a royal woman;

    an elite Brahmin;

    a polished temple dancer;

    an abstract philosophical vision.

It appears through a working village woman performing labour associated with maintaining the household.

The very body that elite visual culture might ignore becomes the template for divinity.
What makes the story ethically complicated

The woman is not named.

Her image is appropriated into theology, while her own:

    voice;

    consent;

    biography;

    family;

    caste;

    interpretation

disappear.

This provides a major documentary chapter:

    Did the legend honour an ordinary woman as the Goddess—or erase her by turning her body into someone else’s revelation?

Historical questions

    What is the earliest printed version of this story?

    Does Kṛṣṇānanda mention it himself?

    Is it present in his family’s genealogy?

    Did it arise in nineteenth-century Bengali popular literature?

    Does an earlier goddess story share the same motif?

    Was the woman originally described as a deity in disguise?

    When did cow-dung work become central to the story?

    Was the account designed to explain existing iconography retrospectively?

The iconography before Kṛṣṇānanda

A responsible film must establish that Kālī did not begin in early modern Bengal.
Older textual features

Earlier sources already associate Kālī or related fierce goddesses with:

    darkness;

    battle;

    dishevelled hair;

    emaciation or terrifying beauty;

    blood;

    death;

    marginal environments;

    weapons;

    garlands of heads or skulls;

    yoginī groups.

Older visual problem

Not every earlier dark fierce goddess can automatically be identified as Kālī.

Museum labels and retrospective scholarship sometimes classify images on the basis of later iconography. Each object needs:

    date;

    provenance;

    inscription;

    attributes;

    original identification where known.

What may genuinely have changed in Bengal

Kṛṣṇānanda’s period may have contributed to stabilizing a particular combination:

    youthful dark female body;

    four arms;

    sword;

    severed head;

    boon and protection gestures;

    necklace of heads;

    girdle of arms;

    extended tongue;

    one foot upon Śiva;

    framing as compassionate Mother.

This combination made fear and intimacy coexist.
Dakṣiṇā Kālī versus other Kālīs
Dakṣiṇā Kālī

Common Bengali iconography presents her:

    with the right foot upon Śiva;

    right hands offering protection and boons;

    left hands carrying a sword and severed head;

    dark or blue-black;

    naked or sky-clad;

    with loose hair;

    wearing severed heads and arms.

She is fierce but considered approachable and protective.
Vāma Kālī

Popular distinctions sometimes describe Vāma Kālī as placing the left foot forward or upon Śiva and being associated more strongly with renunciant or esoteric worship.

These neat oppositions vary between texts and local traditions.
Śmaśāna Kālī

Associated with the cremation ground and more restricted ritual settings.
Siddheśvarī, Rakṣā Kālī and regional forms

Bengal contains numerous Kālī traditions whose images and festival contexts do not fit one universal classification.
Documentary point

Kṛṣṇānanda did not reduce Kālī to one form. His compendium’s significance lies partly in organizing multiplicity.
From frightening deity to loving Mother

The phrase “Kālī became Mother” should not imply that maternal Kālī had no earlier roots.

The shift is one of emphasis.
Ritual Kālī

    approached through mantra and initiation;

    powerful and potentially dangerous;

    connected with specialized practitioners;

    capable of granting liberation and worldly results.

Devotional Kālī

    addressed as Mā, “Mother”;

    scolded, teased and pleaded with;

    invited into domestic emotional life;

    treated as simultaneously terrifying and loving.

Later poetic culmination

Ramprasad Sen’s songs made intimate, emotionally complex Mother Kālī central to Bengali religious culture.

Rather than confidently making Ramprasad Kṛṣṇānanda’s direct student, use this formulation:

    Kṛṣṇānanda’s ritual world and Ramprasad’s devotional world belong to the same broad Bengali transformation in which the tantric Goddess became the intimate Mother.

The Ramprasad chronology problem
Traditional claim

Many popular accounts call Kṛṣṇānanda the guru of Ramprasad Sen.
Chronological difficulty

Ramprasad lived in the eighteenth century.

Depending on which date is accepted, Kṛṣṇānanda may have lived:

    over a century earlier;

    several generations earlier;

    or, under the latest proposed dating, still too early for comfortable direct discipleship.

Possible solutions
A descendant with the same name

A later Kṛṣṇānanda within the family or lineage may have initiated Ramprasad.
Textual guru

Ramprasad may have regarded Kṛṣṇānanda’s work as authoritative without personal contact.
Lineage guru

Religious traditions can call a founding figure “guru” across several generations.
Hagiographic compression

Later biographies may link Bengal’s two greatest Kālī figures to construct a clean succession:

ritual scholar → devotional poet.
Strong video line

    The guru relationship may be spiritually true for the tradition while being biographically impossible.

The Tantrasāra
Do not confuse the two books

There are at least two famous works commonly called Tantrasāra:
Abhinavagupta’s Tantrasāra

A prose condensation of the Tantrāloka, composed in medieval Kashmir.
Kṛṣṇānanda’s Tantrasāra or Bṛhat Tantrasāra

A later Bengali tantric ritual compendium.

Search results frequently mix them, and several commercial or informal sites incorrectly classify Kṛṣṇānanda’s book as Kashmir Śaivism. That error should itself become a short section on digital misinformation.
Meaning

तन्त्रसार — tantrasāra

“The essence of the tantras.”

बृहत् तन्त्रसार — bṛhat tantrasāra

“The great or extensive essence of the tantras.”
Reported scope

Modern Bengali summaries describe the work as synthesizing approximately 170 tantric sources and encompassing:

    Śākta worship;

    Śaiva traditions;

    Gaṇeśa;

    Viṣṇu;

    solar worship;

    mantra;

    deity visualization;

    ritual sequences;

    Mahāvidyā forms;

    domestic and specialized worship.

The exact list should be derived from a reliable edition rather than online summaries.
Why compilation matters

Tantric scriptures are numerous, contradictory and often lineage-specific.

A compiler decides:

    which texts are legitimate;

    which ritual variants to preserve;

    how contradictions are reconciled;

    what is safe or appropriate for whom;

    which deity forms become standard;

    what practitioners can actually use.

Kṛṣṇānanda was therefore not merely copying.

He was editing a religious world.
