Recommended narrative structure
Act I — The woman beside the wall

Tell the vision story without interrupting it.

Let the audience understand why it survives.
Act II — The icon already existed

Introduce older Kālī texts and images.

Reveal that the story cannot literally explain the Goddess’s first appearance.

The question changes:

    What exactly did Kṛṣṇānanda create?

Act III — The invisible compiler

Introduce the historical author and the Tantrasāra.

Show how compilers exercise power by determining which versions survive.
Act IV — The century disappears

Present the four competing timelines.

Use Caitanya and Ramprasad as chronological boundaries.
Act V — Making terror domestic

Explain how Dakṣiṇā Kālī allows:

    frightening iconography;

    protection gestures;

    household worship;

    maternal devotion;

    continued tantric authority.

Act VI — The unnamed woman

Return to the vision.

Ask whether the story:

    elevates ordinary labour;

    appropriates a woman’s body;

    encodes the Goddess appearing beyond elite purity;

    explains an already-existing icon.

Act VII — The impossible disciple

Present the Ramprasad problem.

Show how religious lineages compress time to create ideal succession.
