Research Pack 12: Kubjikā — The Crooked Goddess India Forgot and Nepal Kept Secret
Core video thesis

One of medieval India’s largest Kaula tantric traditions centred on a small, bent or “crooked” goddess called Kubjikā. Her scriptures ran to thousands of verses, mapped the body through alternative chakra systems, and presented her as the supreme coiled power of consciousness. Then her independent worship largely disappeared in India.

Yet the tradition did not die.

Newar initiates in the Kathmandu Valley preserved Kubjikā inside hereditary household lineages, private manuscripts and the broader Sarvāmnāya system—an inclusive Nepalese tantric framework that combines several streams of revelation. Scholars only began documenting the living cult in depth during the late twentieth century, and its secrecy remains part of the tradition rather than merely an accidental absence from scholarship.

The strongest documentary question is:

    Why did one of India’s major tantric goddesses vanish from public memory while Newar families kept her alive behind closed doors?

This is not merely a “hidden goddess” video. It is a story about:

    religious extinction and survival;

    secrecy as preservation;

    India–Nepal transmission;

    the reshaping of Śaiva tantra inside a Newar world;

    alternative chakra systems;

    how a supposedly marginal goddess became central precisely by remaining invisible.

