Kubjikā and the body
Alternative chakra architecture

The Kubjikā system’s five-chakra arrangement shows that chakra maps were designed around specific ritual and theological purposes.

The most useful documentary point is not simply “five instead of seven.”

It is:

    A chakra system is not necessarily an anatomical survey. It is a ritual map built to install a particular divine universe inside a practitioner.

What to compare

Create an evidence-based visual comparison:
Tradition/text	Number and structure	Primary purpose
Kubjikāmatatantra	Five-centre model in studied chapters	Kubjikā-specific ritual/yogic body
Pūrṇānanda	Six centres plus transcendent Sahasrāra	Śākta Kuṇḍalinī ascent
Tibetan systems	Often four, five or six major centres	Completion-stage yogas
Modern wellness	Usually seven equivalent rainbow centres	Psychological/spiritual self-development

Do not imply that each broad tradition has only one map.
Why Nepal preserved her
The Newar manuscript ecosystem

Nepal preserved enormous quantities of Sanskrit Buddhist and Śaiva material through:

    palm-leaf copying;

    hereditary priesthoods;

    family libraries;

    monastery and temple collections;

    ritual use rather than purely antiquarian preservation.

The British Library’s Endangered Archives Programme continues documenting rare manuscripts held in Newar communities, illustrating how religious texts remain distributed across private and lineage collections.
Hereditary initiation

A family system can transmit:

    ritual eligibility;

    deity;

    mantra;

    manuscripts;

    calendrical duties;

    rules of secrecy.

This can preserve a tradition even after its broader public institutions disappear.
Nepalese religious inclusivity

Kubjikā did not have to eliminate other goddesses to survive. The Sarvāmnāya model permits several revelations to coexist as directional or layered aspects of a total system.
Political and geographic stability

The Kathmandu Valley’s distinctive urban and ritual institutions provided continuity through periods when many Indian tantric centres were transformed or destroyed.

Avoid simplifying this to “Muslims destroyed tantra in India.” Religious decline can involve:

    changing patronage;

    assimilation into other goddess traditions;

    manuscript loss;

    shifting social structures;

    reform;

    replacement by more popular deities;

    political disruption from multiple causes.

The secrecy problem
Why secrecy is not merely concealment

Within tantra, secrecy can mean:

    teachings require initiation;

    mantras are efficacious only within lineage;

    divine identity must be ritually installed;

    misunderstanding can distort a practice;

    secrecy separates insider obligation from public curiosity.

What scholarship can document

Scholars can study:

    public manuscripts;

    iconography;

    historical transmission;

    terminology;

    published textual editions;

    voluntarily shared ethnographic information.

What production should not pursue

Do not ask practitioners for:

    restricted mantras;

    initiation formulas;

    private ritual sequences;

    demonstrations;

    confidential lineage names;

    access to closed domestic shrines;

    permission to film simply because another family allowed it.

The film’s conclusion should not be “we finally reveal the secret.”

A better conclusion:

    The fact that something remains undisclosed is itself part of the historical evidence.

