Recommended documentary structure
Act I — The goddess missing from India

Begin with the scale of the textual corpus.

Show thousands of verses and almost no public worship.

Ask:

    How does a religion large enough to produce this literature disappear?

Act II — The Crooked One

Explain her name, contracted body and theology.

Avoid framing her physical form as grotesque.
Act III — The manuscripts in Nepal

Reveal the 1037–38 palm-leaf manuscript.

This moves the story from myth to hard evidence.
Act IV — A different map of the body

Explain the five-centre system.

Contrast it with later standardized chakra charts.
Act V — The Western Transmission

Introduce Kaula directional traditions and the wider Śaiva world.

Connect—but do not collapse—Kubjikā with Trika, Krama and Abhinavagupta’s milieu.
Act VI — India loses the goddess

Present several possible reasons:

    assimilation;

    loss of patronage;

    disappearance of lineages;

    absorption into other Śākta systems;

    manuscript destruction;

    public religious change.

No single-cause explanation should be asserted.
Act VII — Nepal hides her

Explain Newar lineage structure and Sarvāmnāya.

The apparent contradiction becomes the core revelation:

    Secrecy did not cause disappearance. In Nepal, secrecy prevented it.

Act VIII — What can outsiders know?

Show the limits of scholarship and filming.

Distinguish:

    published text;

    family ritual;

    public iconography;

    initiated knowledge.

Ending

    Kubjikā survived because she never needed to become famous.

    India preserved her name in old books. Nepal preserved the social world in which those books could still mean something.

    The crooked goddess remained bent, contracted and hidden—and inside that contraction, an entire medieval religion endured.

