Research Pack 13: Śāntikara — The Tantric King Still Living Beneath Swayambhunath
Core video thesis

Newar Buddhist tradition remembers Śāntikara as a king who renounced political power, became an early Vajrācārya master, built or concealed the sacred core of Swayambhunath, mastered the serpent beings governing Kathmandu’s rainfall, and entered an underground chamber from which he never returned.

Centuries later, when drought threatened the valley, King Pratap Malla was said to have descended through Shantipur’s sealed interior to recover a rain-producing painting associated with the nāgas. The modest temple still stands beside Swayambhunath. Its interior remains restricted, and its mural paintings required emergency conservation following the 2015 earthquake.

The strongest documentary is not simply:

    “A yogi is physically alive under Kathmandu.”

It is:

    How did one hidden tantric become the supernatural architect of Kathmandu’s stupa, water system and Buddhist priesthood—and why does the city continue treating his sealed chamber as a living presence rather than a tomb?

This is one of the best subjects found so far because it unites:

    a surviving restricted temple;

    a medieval Sanskrit/Newar sacred history;

    nāga rain magic;

    royal drought politics;

    the origins of Newar Vajrācārya authority;

    hidden chambers and ritual paintings;

    manuscript-version problems;

    conservation footage;

    and a founder whose historicity is almost impossible to separate from Swayambhunath itself.

