Recommended documentary structure
Act I — The building everyone overlooks

Begin with Swayambhunath’s massive visibility.

Then turn the camera toward the modest sealed temple.

Introduce the claim that Śāntikara remains within it.
Act II — The king who abandoned the city above

Tell the sacred biography:

    royal identity;

    renunciation;

    tantric mastery;

    construction or concealment of Swayambhu.

Clearly label the source as sacred history.
Act III — The light beneath the stupa

Explain the self-arisen dharmadhātu and Śāntikara’s architectural role.

This is the philosophical centre of the first half.
Act IV — The serpent society beneath Kathmandu

Move into water ecology:

    nāgas;

    springs;

    monsoon;

    agriculture;

    rain ritual.

Explain that “weather control” was understood as diplomacy between visible and invisible societies.
Act V — The master disappears underground

Introduce Shantipur’s chambers and living-presence belief.

Present legendary geography separately from surveyed architecture.
Act VI — A second king enters

Move forward to Pratap Malla.

Establish him historically, then tell the drought descent as legend.
Act VII — The rain painting

Investigate:

    what kind of object it may have been;

    whether it survives;

    blood-pigment legend;

    ritual image versus artwork.

Act VIII — The earthquake opens a different history

Use the 2015 damage and mural conservation.

The physical temple finally speaks through:

    pigments;

    plaster;

    architecture;

    conservation records.

Act IX — Why the door remains closed

Do not end with a demand to open the site.

Conclude that the sealed boundary itself maintains:

    lineage authority;

    sacred presence;

    cultural continuity;

    protection of fragile material heritage.

Ending

    Śāntikara may not be physically alive beneath Swayambhunath, and Pratap Malla may never have passed through seven literal doors.

    But Kathmandu still organizes the story of its survival around them: a hidden master below the earth, serpent powers inside the water and a king who must descend before the rain can fall.

    Shantipur is not merely a sealed room. It is the place where the city stores its belief that visible power depends upon invisible relationships.

