Research Pack 14: Bandhudatta — The Tantric Priest Who Captured a God as a Bumblebee
Core video thesis

Newar tradition remembers Bandhudatta as the tantric priest who ended a twelve-year drought by travelling with King Narendradeva to recover the rain deity Karuṇāmaya from a distant tantric kingdom. Unable to carry the god away openly, Bandhudatta compelled him to abandon his body, assume the form of a bumblebee and enter a ritual vessel. Rain reportedly fell the moment the bee was captured.

The priest then created or consecrated the red image now carried through Patan in one of Nepal’s largest chariot festivals. In later versions, the drought exists because Gorakhnāth has immobilized the valley’s rain-serpents and will rise only when his guru Matsyendranāth arrives.

The historical core is far stronger—and stranger—than the miracle alone. Nepal’s oldest surviving chronicle says King Narendradeva and an ācārya named Bandhudatta jointly inaugurated the festival of Bugma Lokeśvara, without calling the deity Matsyendranāth or mentioning Gorakhnāth. Narendradeva is independently attested as a seventh-century ruler, whereas the Nāth elements appear to be later additions.

The video’s central question is:

    Did Bandhudatta historically create Nepal’s great Buddhist rain festival—only for later generations to rewrite his Avalokiteśvara as the Śaiva yogi Matsyendranāth?

This is not merely another Matsyendranāth biography. Bandhudatta gives you a way to show, almost layer by layer, how a Buddhist deity, an indigenous agricultural god, a tantric siddha and a Nāth guru became one figure.
