THE GOD BECAME A BEE

The opening minute must make clear that the bee transformation belongs to later sacred narrative, while the seventh-century royal–priestly festival foundation has considerably stronger historical support.
Opening hook

    Kathmandu had received no rain for twelve years.

    The serpent beings who carried the clouds were trapped beneath the body of a meditating yogi. The king’s armies were useless. The city’s temples could do nothing.

    Only one tantric priest understood the problem.

    Bandhudatta travelled beyond the valley to retrieve the yogi’s guru—the one being powerful enough to make him rise.

    But the god refused to leave. So Bandhudatta forced him out of his body.

    He became a black bumblebee.

    The priest trapped him inside a ritual vessel—and rain immediately began to fall.

Then pull back:

    That is the famous legend. Nepal’s oldest chronicle preserves a shorter and probably earlier story: a real seventh-century king and his ācārya Bandhudatta founded a festival for a deity called Bugma Lokeśvara. Gorakhnāth is nowhere to be found.

Why this is an exceptional subject
1. The miracle is attached to a living festival of enormous scale

The deity Bandhudatta supposedly brought remains the centre of the annual Bunga Dya/Rato Matsyendranāth chariot procession through Lalitpur. The towering wooden chariot is reconstructed and pulled through Patan before the monsoon, while the deity alternates between residences in Patan and Bungamati.
2. The earliest written version is unexpectedly Buddhist

The Gopālarājavaṃśāvalī, whose surviving redaction was completed in the late fourteenth century, records that Narendradeva and Bandhudatta inaugurated the festival of Bugma Lokeśvara. Bandhudatta is called simply an ācārya; the deity is not called Matsyendranāth.
3. A historical king anchors the legend

Locke identifies the relevant Narendradeva as the ruler active approximately 644–680 CE. The king is known from inscriptions and was encountered by a Chinese diplomatic mission in the seventh century. This makes a festival foundation under Narendradeva historically plausible even though the miraculous expedition remains legendary.
4. The story shows religious identity changing over time

The same deity is understood as:

    Bunga Dya, the god of Bungamati;

    Karuṇāmaya, “the Compassionate One”;

    Padmapāṇi or Avalokiteśvara in Newar Buddhism;

    Matsyendranāth or Macchindranāth in Śaiva and Nāth interpretation;

    a rain and agricultural god among farming communities.

The Rubin Museum’s study emphasizes this triple identity and argues that the cult is ancient, possibly extending to or before the seventh century.
5. Bandhudatta may be more historically important than the famous yogis

Matsyendranāth and Gorakhnāth dominate later retellings, but the earliest chronicle foregrounds Bandhudatta and Narendradeva. The priest may therefore represent the real ritual specialist whose work was later absorbed into a larger Nāth mythology.
