Karuṇāmaya

करुणामय — karuṇāmaya

“Made of compassion” or “filled with compassion.”

This is a natural Buddhist epithet for Avalokiteśvara, but in Nepal it also functions as the deity’s intimate local name.
Bunga Dya

The Newar name means approximately:

    “The deity of Bunga,” referring to Bungamati.

It may preserve a local identity older than either the fully Buddhist Avalokiteśvara interpretation or the Nāth name Matsyendranāth. The Rubin Museum describes Bunga Dya as an ancient rain deity particularly associated with Jyāpu farmers.
Matsyendranāth

मत्स्येन्द्रनाथ — matsyendranātha

“Lord of the fish” or “Lord Matsyendra.”

This is the famous Nāth master’s name, but Locke’s chronology suggests it was applied to Bunga Dya substantially later than the festival’s probable seventh-century origin.
The three Bandhudattas
1. The historical ācārya

The figure minimally attested by the oldest chronicle:

    associated with Narendradeva;

    jointly responsible for beginning the Bugma Lokeśvara festival;

    called an ācārya without elaborate biography;

    probably active in the seventh-century valley if the chronicle’s attribution is reliable.

2. The Buddhist tantric master

Later Buddhist accounts present him as:

    Vajrācārya or Bauddhācārya;

    knowledgeable in ritual and mantra;

    capable of invoking or coercing divine beings;

    the master who forms the deity’s image and establishes worship;

    sometimes learned in both Buddhist and non-Buddhist traditions.

3. The syncretic miracle worker

In later expanded narratives he:

    diagnoses Gorakhnāth’s capture of the nāgas;

    identifies Matsyendranāth as Gorakhnāth’s guru;

    travels to Kāmarūpa;

    summons four Bhairavas;

    forces the deity into bee form;

    captures him in a kalaśa;

    brings him to Nepal;

    creates the image and festival.

These layers may represent centuries of additions around a real priestly name.
Evidence grading
Grade A — relatively strong evidence
A tradition connecting Bandhudatta and Narendradeva to the festival is early and persistent

Locke found the association in chronicles, temple hymns and ritual narratives. The oldest chronicle states that the king and his ācārya jointly inaugurated the Bugma Lokeśvara festival.
Narendradeva was a historical seventh-century king

Inscriptional chronology places the relevant ruler around 644–680 CE. Locke argues that this is almost certainly the Narendradeva intended by the chronicles.
The cult of Bunga Dya is ancient

The festival’s Buddhist and agricultural layers appear considerably older than the mature Nāth legend. Current scholarship and museum research describe the cult as extending to the seventh century and perhaps earlier.
The festival and associated institutions remain active

The deity continues to move between Bungamati and Patan, and the annual chariot procession remains one of Lalitpur’s major public rituals.
Bandhudatta was originally remembered as an ācārya, not necessarily a Nāth yogi

The oldest cited record does not call him a Nāth, Aghori or wandering Śaiva ascetic. His later identity as Vajrācārya is more consistent with the Buddhist framing of Bugma Lokeśvara.
Grade B — historically plausible reconstruction
Bandhudatta may have been a court ritual specialist

His partnership with Narendradeva suggests a priest with sufficient authority to:

    organize a major public festival;

    coordinate royal patronage;

    establish an image cult;

    connect urban, agricultural and monastic interests.

The specific institutional title “Vajrācārya” may reflect later terminology, but a Buddhist ritual officiant is plausible.
The festival may have responded to a real drought

Severe monsoon failure would provide a credible reason for establishing or expanding a rain deity’s procession. However, no currently identified seventh-century climate record proves a continuous twelve-year drought.
Bandhudatta may have unified several existing cults

Rather than importing a completely new deity, he may have:

    Buddhistized an older local rain god;

    linked Bungamati’s deity with Avalokiteśvara;

    incorporated royal ritual;

    standardized annual bathing and chariot ceremonies.

This would explain why the deity retains several identities instead of one replacing the others.
The expedition story may encode real transregional ritual exchange

Later accounts place the deity in Kāmarūpa, the Assam region famous in medieval literature for Śaiva, Śākta, Buddhist and yogic tantra. Locke describes Kāmarūpa as a zone where these traditions interacted intensely.

The “journey to Kāmarūpa” may preserve:

    a real pilgrimage;

    the importation of a ritual text or image type;

    foreign initiation;

    political contact;

    or a later claim that the cult derived from tantra’s prestigious eastern homeland.

Grade C — hagiographic narrative

These should be presented explicitly as sacred legend:

    Gorakhnāth physically sat on all the valley’s rain-serpents.

    The drought lasted exactly twelve uninterrupted years.

    Only Matsyendranāth’s presence could make Gorakhnāth stand.

    Bandhudatta summoned four literal Bhairavas to frighten a foreign royal family.

    Matsyendranāth left his body and became a bee.

    Capturing the bee immediately produced rainfall.

    Bandhudatta generated the deity’s physical image solely through mantra.

    Bandhudatta and Narendradeva finally vanished into the image.

    Every ritual detail of the modern festival was established in one seventh-century event.

The bee and image narratives appear in later poetic and ritual versions, not in the oldest brief chronicle notice.
Grade D — claims to avoid
“Bandhudatta kidnapped a real child from Assam”

Some expanded versions turn the deity into a prince or child. This is sacred narrative, not evidence of historical abduction.
“He was definitely Nepal’s first Vajrācārya”

That would require firmer institutional evidence than the chronicle’s generic title ācārya.
“The seventh-century festival was already about Gorakhnāth”

Locke argues the opposite: Gorakhnāth is absent from the earliest records, and the mature drought legend appears later.
“Rato Matsyendranāth is secretly Hindu” or “secretly Buddhist”

The deity functions through overlapping identities. Declaring one interpretation fake misunderstands the Newar cult’s historical structure.
“Bunga Dya is simply the historical yogi Matsyendranāth”

The festival probably predates plausible dates for the Nāth master and the appearance of the name in this cult.
“A bee is still physically sealed inside the statue”

The bee motif concerns divine presence and ritual containment, not a claim verified by opening the image.
Reconstructing the legend

Several versions circulate, and they should be shown side by side rather than merged into one perfect narrative.
Version A — the old chronicle core

    King Narendradeva.

    His ācārya Bandhudatta.

    Foundation of Bugma Lokeśvara’s festival.

    No Gorakhnāth.

    No Matsyendranāth name.

    No bee.

Version B — Avalokiteśvara brought from a sacred mountain

A later Svayaṃbhūpurāṇa recension says Narendradeva, disciple of Bandhudatta, brought Āryāvalokiteśvara from Mount Potalaka or Mount Kalpa because of a twelve-year drought.

This is still predominantly Buddhist:

    Avalokiteśvara;

    Potalaka;

    Buddhist ācārya;

    rain compassion.

Version C — the Kāmarūpa expedition

The deity resides in a distant eastern tantric kingdom. Bandhudatta and the king travel there and must overcome royal resistance.

Depending on the recension:

    the deity is a prince;

    Bandhudatta summons Bhairavas;

    the deity is carried away;

    or he escapes bodily capture by taking another form.

Version D — the bumblebee capture

Bandhudatta induces or compels the deity to leave his body as a bee. The bee enters a kalaśa, rain falls, and the priest later creates the visible image through mantra.
Version E — the Gorakhnāth drought

Gorakhnāth, awaiting his guru, sits upon the nāgas and prevents them from releasing rain. Bandhudatta explains that only the arrival of Matsyendranāth will cause him to rise.

Locke’s historical analysis suggests this Nāth framework was added after Gorakhnāth worship became institutionally visible in the valley from the late fourteenth century onward.
Why a bumblebee?

The bee motif is not random.
Divine presence in miniature

A huge, unmanageable deity compresses into a tiny living point that can enter a vessel.
Sound and mantra

The buzzing of a bee naturally evokes:

    humming;

    vibratory sound;

    seed syllables;

    the audible presence of power.

Pollination and fertility

The bee connects:

    flowering;

    agricultural renewal;

    seasonal abundance;

    the return of life after drought.

Blackness and concealment

A dark bee can disappear visually within the ritual vessel while remaining audible.
Strong narration line

    Bandhudatta does not defeat the god by overpowering his enormous form. He makes infinity small enough to carry.

This is interpretation, not a direct statement from the text.
The kalaśa as portable divinity

कलश — kalaśa

A ritual vessel often associated with:

    water;

    fertility;

    divine presence;

    consecration;

    royal prosperity;

    the containing of an invoked deity.

Capturing the bee in a kalaśa unites the story’s main themes:

    rain inside a water vessel;

    god inside a ritual container;

    distant sacred power made portable;

    the priest controlling access between invisible and visible form.

Visual approach

Animate:

    A bee enters the vessel.

    The vessel becomes a dark monsoon cloud.

    Rain lines descend over the valley.

    The vessel’s outline transforms into the deity’s image.

    The image enters the chariot.

This visually communicates the movement:

living god → compressed presence → image → public festival
The four Bhairavas

Some versions say Bandhudatta summons four Bhairavas and sends them into the foreign city to frighten its king and queen into releasing the divine child.
Why this is interesting

Bandhudatta is represented as Buddhist, yet commands Bhairavas—fierce forms associated strongly with Śaiva tantra and also adapted within Buddhist ritual environments.

This indicates a world where ritual specialists were not constrained by modern textbook boundaries.
Possible meanings

    four directional guardians;

    threatening ritual forces;

    local protective deities;

    Buddhistized Bhairavas;

    later Śaiva colouring added to a Buddhist narrative.

Misleading interpretation to avoid

Do not say:

    “A Buddhist priest secretly practised Hindu black magic.”

Better:

    Medieval Nepalese tantric traditions shared a broad ritual vocabulary in which fierce deities could be reorganized inside Buddhist or Śaiva frameworks.

Bandhudatta and King Narendradeva
The political partnership

The oldest account attributes the festival to both men.

This implies complementary powers:
Narendradeva

    royal authority;

    labour and resources;

    public legitimacy;

    territorial coordination.

Bandhudatta

    ritual knowledge;

    access to divine authority;

    image consecration;

    diagnosis of drought;

    liturgical organization.

Strong thesis line

    The king could command the valley’s population. Bandhudatta could command the being believed to control its rain.

Historical possibility

The festival may have helped Narendradeva:

    reassert rule after political instability;

    unite several settlements;

    bind farming groups to the court;

    integrate Buddhist institutions;

    present monsoon recovery as evidence of legitimate kingship.

This remains inference rather than directly stated evidence.
Why Avalokiteśvara became Matsyendranāth

This is the documentary’s major historical payoff.
Stage 1 — local rain god

A deity tied to Bungamati, farming and rainfall.
Stage 2 — Buddhist Lokeśvara

The deity becomes identified with Avalokiteśvara/Karuṇāmaya, making compassion and Buddhist ritual central.
Stage 3 — tantric siddha associations

The cult absorbs narratives involving yogic powers, eastern tantric lands and miraculous transport.
Stage 4 — Matsyendranāth identification

Śaiva yogis recognize the deity as their guru Matsyendranāth.
Stage 5 — Gorakhnāth enters the drought myth

Gorakhnāth becomes the cause of rain blockage, and the guru’s arrival solves it.

Locke argues that early Bunga Dya texts belonging to the Matsyendranāth school do not mention Gorakhnāth and that institutional evidence for Gorakhnāth worship in the valley emerges in the late fourteenth century.
Why Matsyendra was a perfect bridge

Matsyendranāth already belonged to a fluid world of:

    yoga;

    Kaula tantra;

    siddha mythology;

    eastern India/Kāmarūpa;

    Buddhist and Śaiva attribution;

    rain-making and magical authority.

He could be inserted without fully displacing Avalokiteśvara.
Kāmarūpa: the tantric homeland

Later versions locate the god in Kāmarūpa, broadly corresponding to Assam around Guwahati.

Locke describes medieval Kāmarūpa as a major contact zone for:

    Śaivism;

    Śāktism;

    Buddhism;

    yoga;

    indigenous traditions;

    secret ritual knowledge.

Why the setting matters

To a Nepalese audience, Kāmarūpa could function as:

    a dangerous foreign kingdom;

    a land of powerful women and goddesses;

    tantra’s eastern source;

    the place where lost divine power must be recovered.

Field-research angle

Investigate whether any Assamese source preserves a reverse tradition:

    a deity taken from Kāmarūpa to Nepal;

    Bandhudatta;

    Narendradeva;

    Matsyendranāth leaving Assam;

    a divine prince becoming a bee.

The absence of an Assamese counterpart would suggest that Kāmarūpa functions mainly as Nepalese sacred geography.
