Research Pack 15: Caurangināth / Puran Bhagat — The Dismembered Prince Who Became Punjab’s Immortal Yogi
Core video thesis

One of Punjab’s darkest and most widespread saint legends begins with a prince falsely accused by his young stepmother, condemned by his own father, mutilated and thrown into a well outside Sialkot.

Years later, Gorakhnāth hears a voice from the abandoned well, restores the prince and initiates him as the Nāth siddha Caurangināth—“the lord associated with four limbs.” The revived prince refuses revenge, forgives the family that destroyed him and becomes a healer whose shrine attracts people seeking children and cures.

The story travelled extraordinarily far:

    Muslim Punjabi poet Qādir Yār made it one of the great nineteenth-century qissas;

    Nāths identified Puran with Caurangināth, one of their major siddhas;

    Pakistani Muslims continued visiting “Puran’s Well” near Sialkot;

    Hindu refugee communities carried his soil and cult across Partition into Jammu and Indian Punjab;

    Punjabi poet Shiv Kumar Batalvi retold the story from the accused stepmother Lūṇā’s perspective, transforming the supposed villain into a victim of caste, patriarchy and forced marriage.

The strongest documentary question is:

    Was Puran Bhagat originally a Punjabi prince absorbed into the Nāth tradition, or was the ancient siddha Caurangināth localized as the lost son of Sialkot?

There is strong evidence for a long-lived Punjabi literary, oral and shrine tradition, but virtually no secure evidence that Puran, King Sālvān or the mutilation episode are datable biography. The well is real; the saint’s identity is historically layered.
