Thumbnail text:

THROWN INTO THE WELL

Alternative:

GORAKHNĀTH HEARD HIM

The physical punishment belongs to legendary narrative. Use stylized shadows, manuscript art or an empty well rather than graphic reenactment.
Opening hook

    Outside Sialkot stands an old well associated with a prince whose own father ordered him destroyed.

    His young stepmother accused him of attacking her after he refused her advances. Soldiers took him into the forest, severed his limbs and threw him into the darkness.

    He remained there until the yogi Gorakhnāth passed nearby and heard a human voice rising from below.

    Gorakhnāth pulled him from the well, restored his body and gave him another name: Caurangināth.

    The prince eventually returned to the palace—but not for revenge.

Then qualify:

    This is the story Punjab sang. The historical record begins much later, in poetry, shrine memory and competing Nāth genealogies.

Why this subject is exceptional
1. It is a major Nāth story almost invisible globally

Puran Bhagat remains culturally recognizable in Punjab, Pakistan and parts of northern India, while English-language yoga history usually reduces Caurangināth to one name in a list of siddhas.

The story gives Caurangināth:

    a birthplace;

    parents;

    a moral crisis;

    a social world;

    a reason for renunciation;

    a physical landscape;

    a shrine;

    a dramatic relationship with Gorakhnāth.

Whether those belong to the earliest Caurangināth tradition is the central historical problem.
2. A Muslim poet preserved a Hindu yogi’s definitive epic

Qādir Yār’s Qissa Puran Bhagat became one of the most important literary tellings. Its Punjabi text is publicly accessible in Gurmukhi and Shahmukhi-oriented archives and is marked public domain in the Internet Archive copy.
3. The well remains a shared sacred location in Pakistan

Puran’s Well—Pūran dā Khūh—is still identified near Sialkot. Pakistani reporting describes continuing visits and local claims concerning the site, while archaeological officials have discussed the difficulty of separating the physical monument from legend.
4. Partition split the saint’s sacred geography

Hindu communities displaced from the Sialkot region established new Puran/Sahaj Nāth shrines in Jammu and neighbouring areas. Community traditions say soil from the Pakistani shrine was carried into India.

This creates a powerful modern story:

    A saint associated with bodily fragmentation became fragmented geographically by Partition.

5. A modern Punjabi masterpiece put the villain on trial

Shiv Kumar Batalvi’s Lūṇā retold the legend from the young stepmother’s perspective. Rather than treating her as a simple temptress, the play asks what happens when a low-status young woman is married to an old king and then condemned for desiring someone her own age.

Batalvi received the Sahitya Akademi Award for Lūṇā, making the reinterpretation part of modern Punjabi literary history.
