The name may connect him to the broader siddha and Nāth vocabulary of spontaneous realization, but the historical relationship between “Sahaj Nāth” and “Caurangināth” needs local verification.
The central historical problem

There are at least three overlapping figures.
1. Caurangināth, the pan-Indian siddha

Caurangināth appears in Nāth and siddha lists, Sanskrit or vernacular yogic attribution, sculpture programmes and regional lineages.

In some eastern traditions, he is connected with Bengal rather than Sialkot. Some lists make him a royal son of eastern India, while Punjabi tradition makes him Puran, son of Sālvān of Sialkot.
2. Puran Bhagat, the Punjabi saint-prince

This figure belongs to the Sialkot cycle:

    King Sālvān;

    Queen Icchrā or Ichhrān;

    young Queen Lūṇā;

    astrologically imposed isolation;

    false accusation;

    well;

    Gorakhnāth;

    restored saint;

    Prince Rasālū.

3. Sahaj Nāth, community ancestor and healer

This figure is worshipped by particular communities and connected with:

    fertility;

    protection;

    ancestral identity;

    post-Partition shrines;

    seasonal communal gatherings.

The three may represent one evolving tradition, but they should not be automatically collapsed.
Evidence grading
Grade A — strongly documented reception history
Puran Bhagat is a major Punjabi qissa subject

Numerous Punjabi poets composed versions of the story. A modern literary study notes that at least twelve writers between Kishan Singh Arif in the nineteenth century and Balak Ram Jogishwar in the twentieth wrote Puran Bhagat qissas.
Qādir Yār’s text survives and is publicly accessible

The Punjabi poem survives in digital scans and online transcriptions. The Internet Archive item is marked Public Domain Mark 1.0.

