Qādir Yār and the Muslim Punjabi Puran
Who was Qādir Yār?

Qādir Yār was a nineteenth-century Punjabi poet writing in the qissa tradition. His Puran Bhagat became one of the most influential versions of the story and is associated with the cultural world of the Sikh Empire. Recent biographical writing dates him approximately 1802–1892, although the exact chronology should be checked through Pakistani Punjabi scholarship.
Why his version matters

A Muslim poet presents:

    Hindu kings;

    Nāth yogis;

    fate;

    moral purity;

    renunciation;

    divine justice

as shared Punjabi inheritance.

The text demonstrates that Puran’s story was not confined to one sectarian community.
