Recommended documentary structure
Act I — The voice beneath Sialkot

Begin at the well.

Tell Gorakhnāth’s discovery before explaining how Puran arrived there.

This creates immediate mystery:

    Who was speaking from the bottom?

Act II — The prince hidden from his father

Tell the birth prophecy and twelve-year isolation.

Establish the court’s distorted family structure.
Act III — Lūṇā

Tell the traditional accusation.

Then pause and reconstruct Lūṇā’s own position:

    age;

    caste;

    forced marriage;

    lack of power.

Do not resolve the moral problem too quickly.
Act IV — The king’s irreversible judgment

Present the legendary sentence without graphic imagery.

The king’s failure, not only Lūṇā’s accusation, should remain central.
Act V — Gorakhnāth remakes the body

The rescue becomes:

    miracle;

    initiation;

    second birth;

    explanation of Caurangināth’s name.

Act VI — Was Puran already a different siddha?

Introduce eastern Caurangināth traditions, Bhojpuri attribution and conflicting royal origins.

This is the main historical twist.
Act VII — A Muslim poet preserves the yogi

Introduce Qādir Yār and the multi-religious Punjabi qissa world.
Act VIII — Lūṇā speaks back

Move to Batalvi’s reinterpretation.

Show how modern Punjab placed the old legend itself on trial.
Act IX — Partition divides the saint

Move from Sialkot to Indian shrines.

Show one tradition living across an inaccessible border.
Ending

    History cannot prove that Puran was a prince, that Gorakhnāth restored his body or even that Puran and Caurangināth began as the same man.

    But Punjab kept returning to the story because every age found a different wound inside it: betrayal, failed justice, caste, forbidden desire, exile and the possibility that a broken life could acquire another name.

    Puran’s miracle was not merely that his body returned. It was that the identity created by violence did not become the person he remained.

