Research Pack 16: Ānand Nāth of Jakhbar — The Yogi Who Sold Alchemical Mercury to Aurangzeb
Core video thesis

In 1661, the Mughal emperor Aurangzeb reportedly wrote to an obscure Nāth yogi named Ānand Nāth—not to threaten him, convert him or destroy his monastery, but to complain about the quality of mercury the yogi had supplied.

The letter addresses Ānand Nāth with conspicuous respect, invokes Śiva’s favour, asks him to refine and send more mercury, dispatches money and cloth, and promises imperial protection. Other surviving documents show that Jakhbar’s Nāth establishment received or renewed grants under Akbar, Jahangir, Shah Jahan and Aurangzeb. A later Aurangzeb-era order, dated 1682 in the published collection, reportedly reaffirmed revenue support even after the emperor had reinstated the jizya tax.

This does not prove that Aurangzeb secretly converted to Nāth yoga, drank an immortality elixir or became Ānand Nāth’s disciple. Nāth legend later recast him variously as persecutor, defeated tyrant and awkward would-be devotee. The documents instead reveal something more historically useful:

    A powerful Mughal ruler and a Śaiva yogi-monastery participated in a practical economy of patronage, protection, medicine, alchemy and political negotiation that modern Hindu-versus-Muslim narratives struggle to accommodate.

The strongest documentary question is:

    Why was one of India’s most controversial Muslim emperors purchasing purified mercury from a Nāth yogi—and what did either side believe that mercury could do?

