Thumbnail text:

AURANGZEB’S ALCHEMIST

Alternative:

THE MERCURY WAS BAD

The second is oddly compelling because it derives from the letter’s practical complaint rather than an invented supernatural claim.
Opening hook

    In 1661, Aurangzeb received a parcel from a Hindu yogi.

    Inside were two tolas of mercury.

    The emperor was not satisfied. He wrote back to the yogi, respectfully invoked Śiva’s favour and asked him to purify a better batch without unnecessary delay. He sent cloth, twenty-five rupees and orders that the yogi’s monastery remain protected.

    The recipient was Ānand Nāth, head of a Nāth establishment at Jakhbar.

    So why was the most controversial Mughal emperor in Indian history buying refined mercury from a Śaiva ascetic?

Then establish the boundary:

    The letter is documentary evidence. Claims that it made Aurangzeb immortal, cured a secret disease or converted him to Nāth religion are not.

Why this subject is unusually strong
1. The central object is an archival document

Unlike most siddha stories, this subject begins with Persian administrative and personal documents collected, transcribed and translated by B. N. Goswamy and J. S. Grewal in The Mughals and the Jogis of Jakhbar. The volume reproduces a longer history of grants and correspondence connecting the monastery with several Mughal rulers.
2. Mercury opens an entire hidden history

Mercury—pārada in Sanskrit—occupied overlapping worlds:

    medicine;

    metallurgy;

    pigment and craft production;

    testing of metals;

    courtly experimentation;

    Indian alchemy or rasaśāstra;

    Nāth and siddha traditions;

    claims about bodily transformation and longevity.

The letter proves a material exchange. It does not specify which one of these purposes Aurangzeb intended.
3. The relationship lasted beyond one transaction

The Jakhbar material reportedly contains grants and orders associated with four generations of Mughal rule. Akbar granted land to the monastery’s head Uddant or Udant Nāth; Jahangir replaced or renewed support after the original land was affected; Shah Jahan issued another grant in 1642; Aurangzeb continued protection and later revenue arrangements.
4. Nāth folklore remembers a completely different Aurangzeb

In later Nāth narratives, Aurangzeb may appear as:

    a destructive imperial enemy;

    a ruler humbled by a yogi’s powers;

    a clumsy or inadequate devotee;

    a symbolic “badshah” rather than the precise historical individual.

Véronique Bouillier argues that these contradictory roles expose the Nāths’ complex relationship with Islam; the emperor functions partly as a narrative symbol of worldly power confronting yogic power.
5. It punctures two simplistic political stories simultaneously

The evidence does not support either extreme:

    “Aurangzeb indiscriminately destroyed every non-Muslim institution.”

    “Aurangzeb was uniformly tolerant and temple-friendly.”

His reign included coercion, selective destruction, legal discrimination, alliances, grants and pragmatic protection. Jakhbar demonstrates that imperial policy was shaped by local politics, utility, inherited commitments and relationships with particular institutions—not one consistent slogan. Modern scholarship emphasizes that public memory often reduces this complicated ruler to a one-dimensional symbol.
Who was Ānand Nāth?
What is documentable

Ānand Nāth was the head or senior figure of the Jakhbar Nāth establishment during the opening years of Aurangzeb’s reign.

He is connected with:

    imperial correspondence;

    the supply or preparation of mercury;

    a monastery already possessing Mughal patronage;

    requests for protection;

    the institutional lineage of earlier Jakhbar mahants.

The documents provide more evidence for his political and economic role than for his inner spiritual biography.
What remains unknown

Publicly accessible materials currently do not securely establish:

    his birth date;

    family;

    initiation guru;

    exact Nāth sub-lineage;

    writings;

    philosophical position;

    alchemical recipes;

    whether he personally processed the mercury;

    whether he travelled to the imperial court;

    when or where he died;

    whether a tomb or samādhi survives.

Name

आनन्दनाथ — ānandanātha

    ānanda: bliss;

    nātha: lord, protector or master.

Approximately:

    “Lord of Bliss.”

This is likely an initiated Nāth name rather than a birth name.
Jakhbar: the monastery history nearly forgot
Location problem

Jakhbar is identified with a settlement in the wider Kathua–Pathankot–Punjab hill-border region. Modern gazetteers and mapping sources place a Jakhbar in Kathua district at approximately 32.377° N, 75.453° E. The precise relationship between the modern settlement, the original grant lands and the historical monastery must be established through field research rather than assumed from the name alone.
Why it mattered

The Jakhbar monastery appears to have occupied a strategic zone linking:

    Punjab plains;

    Jammu hill states;

    routes toward Kashmir;

    Pathankot;

    Mughal administrative territory;

    travelling ascetic networks.

Its power may have derived not only from spirituality but from:

    land;

    agricultural revenue;

    movement of pilgrims;

    medicinal knowledge;

    mediation between local populations and state officials;

    mobility across difficult border terrain.

Strong visual thesis

    Jakhbar was not a remote hut receiving charity from emperors. It was an institution valuable enough for successive rulers to keep negotiating with it.

