Aurangzeb corresponded with Ānand Nāth about mercury

The published letter says that two tolas of mercury had arrived but did not meet expectation; Ānand Nāth was asked to refine more carefully and send it promptly. The emperor also sent cloth and twenty-five rupees and directed an official to arrange the yogi’s protection.
Jakhbar received Mughal patronage before Aurangzeb

The documentary sequence traces support through Akbar, Jahangir and Shah Jahan, including land grants or renewals.
Aurangzeb-era support did not end with the mercury letter

A document dated 1682 in the published discussion reportedly restored or confirmed madad-i-maʿāsh support and provided annual revenue to the Nāth establishment. This was after Aurangzeb’s 1679 restoration of the jizya, illustrating that restrictive general policy and particular institutional patronage could coexist.
Nāth–Muslim encounters were historically fluid

Bouillier’s open-access study documents shared vocabulary, narratives and doctrinal interaction between Nāth yogis and Muslims, including materials such as the Mohammad Bodh. She argues that Nāth identity often crossed hard Hindu–Muslim boundaries more easily than modern communal histories imply.

