Grade B — plausible historical inference
Ānand Nāth possessed specialist alchemical or medical expertise

The request for refined mercury implies access to technical knowledge or skilled personnel. Nāth institutions were historically associated with bodily disciplines, healing and rasa traditions.

However, the monastery might also have functioned as an intermediary purchasing mercury from someone else.
Aurangzeb trusted the monastery’s product enough to order another batch

The emperor’s dissatisfaction was with quality, not with the premise of obtaining mercury from Ānand Nāth. This implies an already established expectation that the yogi could supply a superior product.
The emperor may have wanted medicinally processed mercury

Processed mercury was used in several early modern medical and alchemical traditions. Possible purposes include:

    court medicine;

    compound preparation;

    metallurgical experimentation;

    pigments;

    testing or purification of precious metals;

    curiosity about alchemy.

The letter itself must be checked for terms indicating the purpose. Until then, “Aurangzeb’s immortality elixir” is unjustified.
Ānand Nāth may have used the exchange to renew institutional security

A monastery negotiating with the emperor could exchange specialized knowledge or goods for:

    revenue protection;

    recognition of old land rights;

    intervention against hostile officials;

    gifts;

    prestige.

This would be a normal political transaction as much as a spiritual encounter.
Grade C — Nāth hagiography and later storytelling

Claims to introduce as legend rather than documentary fact may include:

    Ānand Nāth demonstrated supernatural mercury before Aurangzeb.

    The emperor became his disciple.

    Aurangzeb attempted to test or deceive him.

    The yogi rendered weapons ineffective.

    Aurangzeb attacked the monastery and was magically defeated.

    The emperor received miraculous healing.

    Ānand Nāth granted or refused immortality.

    The yogi’s mercury could transform base metal into gold.

    Aurangzeb secretly revered Śiva.

    Imperial grants were apologies after miraculous humiliation.

Bouillier’s work shows that later Nāth stories commonly transform Aurangzeb into a symbolic opponent or failed devotee. They reveal Nāth self-understanding, not necessarily court history.
Grade D — misleading claims to avoid
“Aurangzeb converted to Hinduism”

No evidence in the Jakhbar letters supports this.
“Ānand Nāth was Aurangzeb’s personal guru”

Respectful court language and material exchange do not establish formal discipleship.
“The mercury was definitely an immortality drug”

The purpose is unresolved.
“The letter proves Aurangzeb was religiously tolerant”

One protected monastery cannot define a forty-nine-year reign.
“The letter proves stories about temple destruction are false”

Protection and destruction could occur under the same ruler in different contexts.
“Aurangzeb hated all Nāths”

The Jakhbar evidence contradicts that universal statement, not every conflict tradition.
“Nāth alchemy was primitive chemistry”

Rasaśāstra combined pharmacology, metallurgy, ritual, cosmology and body-transformation theory. It should neither be dismissed as nonsense nor represented as equivalent to modern laboratory science.
“Purified mercury is safe”

Mercury is toxic. The documentary should remain historical and must not describe processing, ingestion, dosage or experimentation.
The mercury letter
Reported translation

The widely reproduced English/Hindi rendering contains the following major points:

    Ānand Nāth is addressed as possessing elevated powers and as a revered figure.

    Aurangzeb wishes him peace under Śiva’s grace.

    The emperor acknowledges receiving two tolas of mercury.

    The mercury was not as good as expected from the yogi’s claims.

    Ānand Nāth is asked to refine more carefully.

    He should send it without unnecessary delay.

    Cloth and twenty-five rupees are sent.

    Fateh Chand is instructed to arrange protection.

    The yogi may write if he requires imperial assistance.

Important translation questions

Before scripting exact quotations, inspect the Persian transcription and plates.

Ask:

    What Persian word is translated as “mercury”?

    What word is translated as “refine” or “purify”?

    Does “two tolas” appear unambiguously?

    Is “Śiva-mūrti” part of the formal address?

    Is “by the grace of Śiva-jī” literal?

    Is the letter wholly in Aurangzeb’s name or prepared by a secretary?

    Is it sealed, signed or endorsed?

    What date system appears?

    Does the tone match conventional correspondence with holy figures?

    Does “very confidential” appear in the original or editorial translation?

Strong documentary beat

Show the Persian first, then the translation.

The visual shock should not be:

    “Muslim emperor says Śiva!”

It should be:

    Imperial Persian administration was capable of addressing a Śaiva ascetic through terminology appropriate to the recipient rather than through one rigid vocabulary.

