Historical possibilities
Medicine

Mercury could enter carefully compounded remedies within some medical systems.
Metallurgy

It could be used in amalgamation and work with metals.
Assaying and craft

Mercury had value in working with precious substances and pigments.
Alchemical experimentation

Some rasa literature connected processed mercury with:

    bodily stability;

    longevity;

    extraordinary resilience;

    transmutation.

These claims belong to historical alchemical theory, not established modern medical fact.
Imperial curiosity

Mughal courts collected:

    medicines;

    unusual minerals;

    rare substances;

    technical expertise;

    information from ascetics and specialists.

Aurangzeb’s interest need not imply personal ingestion.
The unanswered question

The surviving summary tells us what was purchased, but not clearly why.

That uncertainty should drive the video rather than being filled with speculation.
Why would Nāths know alchemy?
The siddha body

Nāth traditions often treat the body not as a prison to abandon immediately, but as a site to discipline, stabilize and transform.

Themes include:

    mastery of breath;

    reversal of decay;

    conserving vital essences;

    bodily perfection;

    liberation while embodied;

    amaratva, immortality or death-transcendence in several senses.

Mercury as a material analogue

In Indian alchemical imagination, mercury can appear:

    mobile and difficult to fix;

    powerful but unstable;

    transformable through repeated treatment;

    capable of binding with other substances.

The yogic body is similarly treated as unstable matter that practice must “fix.”
Strong philosophical line

    Nāth alchemy imagined the laboratory and the body as mirrors: mercury had to be stabilized just as consciousness and vital force had to be stabilized.

This is an interpretive synthesis. Attribute technical historical claims to specialist scholarship during scripting.
The four-emperor relationship
Akbar

The Jakhbar documents reportedly record a 1571 land grant of approximately two hundred bighas in Bhōā or a similarly named village to Mahant Udant Nāth after Akbar’s visit.
Jahangir

When the original grant was affected—described in some summaries as resulting from a natural event or changed land conditions—Jahangir issued replacement or renewed support.
Shah Jahan

A 1642 grant reportedly continued roughly comparable assistance.
Aurangzeb

    1661 mercury correspondence and protection.

    further sealed letters preserved in the collection;

    1682 revenue or grant confirmation.

Documentary significance

The monastery was not the personal eccentricity of one “tolerant” emperor.

It had become an institution whose relationship with the Mughal state survived dynastic succession.
Aurangzeb in Nāth legend

Bouillier distinguishes historical documents from Nāth narrative memory.
Aurangzeb as persecutor

He embodies:

    imperial force;

    religious hostility;

    threat to monasteries;

    the worldly king who believes soldiers outrank siddhis.

Aurangzeb as humbled ruler

The yogi defeats or exposes him, demonstrating:

    spiritual power over military power;

    the immortality of Nāth tradition;

    the inability of political rule to command realized beings.

Aurangzeb as clumsy devotee

Some stories make him attempt devotion without understanding Nāth discipline. He becomes less a villain than an inadequate aspirant.
Aurangzeb as generic badshah

The historical name may function as shorthand for “the Muslim emperor.” Episodes from different rulers or periods can accumulate around him because he became the most powerful available symbol of antagonistic kingship.
Strong narrative question

    Are the stories remembering Aurangzeb—or using Aurangzeb’s name to explain how yogis survive every empire?

Nāths and Islam beyond Jakhbar

The mercury letter is not an isolated anomaly.
Gorakhṭrī

Babur recorded visiting the famous yogi centre at Gorakhṭrī, in present-day Peshawar, in 1519. He was reportedly unimpressed by the cramped or disorderly dwellings, but his visit demonstrates the centre’s wide reputation. Later Akbar commissioned images associated with Babur’s encounter.
Tilla Jogian

Akbar visited or patronized Nāth centres in the Jhelum region. Colonial gazetteers reported the preservation of old Mughal grant documents at Tilla Jogian into the twentieth century.
Shared religious language

Nāth texts and oral traditions sometimes adopt Islamic names, symbols and concepts, while Muslim writers incorporated yogic knowledge into Persian and Arabic works. Bouillier’s study emphasizes that the relationship included debate, borrowing and translation rather than only conflict.
Amṛtakuṇḍa

A Sanskrit or Indic yogic work circulated through Persian and Arabic translations, becoming an important vehicle for yogic concepts in Islamic intellectual environments. Carl Ernst’s research documents this “Islamization of yoga” as translation and reinterpretation rather than simple conversion.
